(function(){(window.PhysicaNeetYear=window.PhysicaNeetYear||{})[2019]=[{id:"neet-2019-q001",pn:1,y:"NEET 2019 \xB7 5 May",tp:"Plant Kingdom",st:"Life cycle patterns",q:"From evolutionary point of view, retention of the female gametophyte with developing young embryo on the parent sporophyte for some time, is first observed in :",o:["Pteridophytes","Gymnosperms","Liverworts","Mosses"],c:0,s:`In pteridophytes the female gametophyte stays on the parent sporophyte for some time.
The zygote grows into a young embryo inside it.
This is the first step towards the seed habit.
So the answer is Pteridophytes.`,sub:"Botany",ch:"Plant Kingdom"},{id:"neet-2019-q002",pn:2,y:"NEET 2019 \xB7 5 May",tp:"Human Reproduction",st:"Fertilisation",q:"Extrusion of second polar body from egg nucleus occurs :",o:["Before entry of sperm into ovum","Simultaneously with first cleavage","After entry of sperm but before fertilization","After fertilization"],c:2,s:`The secondary oocyte is stuck at metaphase II until a sperm enters.
Sperm entry triggers completion of meiosis II.
This releases the second polar body.
Fusion of the two nuclei (fertilization) comes after this.`,sub:"Zoology",ch:"Human Reproduction"},{id:"neet-2019-q003",pn:3,y:"NEET 2019 \xB7 5 May",tp:"Biotechnology: Principles and Processes",st:"Isolation of DNA",q:"DNA precipitation out of a mixture of bimolecules can be achieved by treatment with :",o:["Methanol at room temperature","Chilled chloroform","Isopropanol","Chilled ethanol"],c:3,s:`DNA is soluble in water but not in cold ethanol.
Adding chilled ethanol makes DNA come out as fine threads.
These threads can be spooled out.`,sub:"Botany",ch:"Biotechnology: Principles and Processes"},{id:"neet-2019-q004",pn:4,y:"NEET 2019 \xB7 5 May",tp:"Breathing and Exchange of Gases",st:"Respiratory disorders",q:"Due to increasing air-borne allergens and pollutants, many people in urban areas are suffering from respiratory disorder causing wheezing due to :",o:["Proliferation of fibrous tissues and damage of the alveolar walls","Reduction in the secretion of surfactants by pneumocytes","Benign growth on mucous lining of nasal cavity","Inflammation of bronchi and bronchioles"],c:3,s:`Wheezing with difficulty in breathing is the sign of asthma.
Asthma is an allergic disorder.
It is caused by inflammation of bronchi and bronchioles.`,sub:"Zoology",ch:"Breathing and Exchange of Gases"},{id:"neet-2019-q005",pn:5,y:"NEET 2019 \xB7 5 May",tp:"Biodiversity and Conservation",st:"Conservation efforts",q:"The Earth summit held in Rio de Janeiro in 1992 was called :",o:["to assess threat posed to native species by invasive weed species","for immediate steps to discontinue use of CFCs that were damaging the ozone layer","to reduce CO2 emissions and global warming","for conservation of biodiversity and sustainable utilization of its benefits"],c:3,s:`The 1992 Rio Earth Summit was a call to all nations.
Its aim was to conserve biodiversity and use its benefits sustainably.`,sub:"Botany",ch:"Biodiversity and Conservation"},{id:"neet-2019-q006",pn:6,y:"NEET 2019 \xB7 5 May",tp:"Evolution",st:"Human evolution",q:`Match the hominids with their correct brain size :
(a) Homo habilis (i) 900 cc
(b) Homo neanderthalensis (ii) 1350 cc
(c) Homo erectus (iii) 650-800 cc
(d) Homo sapiens (iv) 1400 cc
Select the correct option.`,o:["(a)-(iii), (b)-(iv), (c)-(i), (d)-(ii)","(a)-(iv), (b)-(iii), (c)-(i), (d)-(ii)","(a)-(iii), (b)-(i), (c)-(iv), (d)-(ii)","(a)-(iii), (b)-(ii), (c)-(i), (d)-(iv)"],c:0,s:`Brain sizes: Homo habilis 650-800 cc.
Homo erectus about 900 cc.
Homo sapiens about 1350 cc.
Neanderthal man about 1400 cc.
So (a)-(iii), (b)-(iv), (c)-(i), (d)-(ii).`,sub:"Zoology",ch:"Evolution"},{id:"neet-2019-q007",pn:7,y:"NEET 2019 \xB7 5 May",tp:"Chemical Coordination and Integration",st:"Mechanism of hormone action",q:"How does steroid hormone influence the cellular activities ?",o:["Activating cyclic AMP located on the cell membrane","Using aquaporin channels as second messenger","Changing the permeability of the cell membrane","Binding to DNA and forming a gene-hormone complex"],c:3,s:`Steroid hormones are lipid soluble and enter the cell.
They bind intracellular receptors.
The hormone-receptor complex acts on the genome and changes gene expression.`,sub:"Zoology",ch:"Chemical Coordination and Integration"},{id:"neet-2019-q008",pn:8,y:"NEET 2019 \xB7 5 May",tp:"Molecular Basis of Inheritance",st:"Human Genome Project",q:"Expressed Sequence Tags (ESTs) refers to :",o:["DNA polymorphism","Novel DNA sequences","Genes expressed as RNA","Polypeptide expression"],c:2,s:`In the Human Genome Project, genes that are expressed as RNA were identified as ESTs.
This is one of the two main approaches of genome annotation.`,sub:"Botany",ch:"Molecular Basis of Inheritance"},{id:"neet-2019-q009",pn:9,y:"NEET 2019 \xB7 5 May",tp:"Plant Growth and Development",st:"Plant growth regulators",q:"It takes very long time for pineapple plants to produce flowers. Which combination of hormones can be applied to artificially induce flowering in pineapple plants throughout the year to increase yield ?",o:["Gibberellin and Abscicic acid","Cytokinin and Abscisic acid","Auxin and Ethylene","Gibberellin and Cytokinin"],c:2,s:`Auxin and ethylene can induce flowering in pineapple.
They make flowering happen all year round and raise the yield.`,sub:"Botany",ch:"Plant Growth and Development"},{id:"neet-2019-q010",pn:10,y:"NEET 2019 \xB7 5 May",tp:"Ecosystem",st:"Ecological pyramids",q:"Which of the following ecological pyramids is generally inverted?",o:["Pyramid of biomass in a forest","Pyramid of biomass in a sea","Pyramid of numbers in grassland","Pyramid of energy"],c:1,s:`In the sea, producers are tiny phytoplankton with a small standing biomass.
The consumers (fishes) have a larger biomass.
So the biomass pyramid in a sea is inverted.
The energy pyramid is always upright.`,sub:"Botany",ch:"Ecosystem"},{id:"neet-2019-q011",pn:11,y:"NEET 2019 \xB7 5 May",tp:"Cell: The Unit of Life",st:"Cell organelles",q:"Which of the following pair of organelles does not contains DNA ?",o:["Lysosomes and Vacuoles","Nuclear envelope and Mitochondria","Mitochodria and Lysosomes","Chloroplast and Vacuoles"],c:0,s:`Mitochondria and chloroplasts have their own DNA.
Lysosomes and vacuoles have no DNA.
So the pair with no DNA is lysosomes and vacuoles.`,sub:"Botany",ch:"Cell: The Unit of Life"},{id:"neet-2019-q012",pn:12,y:"NEET 2019 \xB7 5 May",tp:"Human Reproduction",st:"Male reproductive system",q:"Select the correct sequence for transport of sperm cells in male reproductive system :",o:["Seminiferous tubules \u2192 Vasa efferentia \u2192 Epididymis \u2192 Inguinal canal \u2192 Urethra","Testis \u2192 Epididymis \u2192 Vasa efferentia \u2192 Vas deferens \u2192 Ejaculatory duct \u2192 Inguinal canal \u2192 Urethra \u2192 Urethral meatus","Testis \u2192 Epididymis \u2192 Vasa efferentia \u2192 Rete testis \u2192 Inguinal canal \u2192 Urethra","Seminiferous tubules \u2192 Rete testis \u2192 Vasa efferentia \u2192 Epididymis \u2192 Vas deferens \u2192 Ejaculatory duct \u2192 Urethra \u2192 Urethral meatus"],c:3,s:`Sperms form in seminiferous tubules.
They pass to rete testis, then vasa efferentia, then epididymis.
Then they go through vas deferens and ejaculatory duct to the urethra.
They leave the body through the urethral meatus.`,sub:"Zoology",ch:"Human Reproduction"},{id:"neet-2019-q013",pn:13,y:"NEET 2019 \xB7 5 May",tp:"Chemical Coordination and Integration",st:"Disorders of endocrine glands",q:`Match the following hormones with the respective disease :
(a) Insulin (i) Addison\u2019s disease
(b) Thyroxin (ii) Diabetes insipidus
(c) Corticoids (iii) Acromegaly
(d) Growth Hormones (iv) Goitre
(v) Diabetes mellitus
Select the correct option.`,o:["(a)-(v), (b)-(iv), (c)-(i), (d)-(iii)","(a)-(ii), (b)-(iv), (c)-(i), (d)-(iii)","(a)-(v), (b)-(i), (c)-(ii), (d)-(iii)","(a)-(ii), (b)-(iv), (c)-(iii), (d)-(i)"],c:0,s:`Lack of insulin causes diabetes mellitus.
Lack of iodine means less thyroxine and goitre.
Low corticoids cause Addison\u2019s disease.
Excess growth hormone in adults causes acromegaly.
So (a)-(v), (b)-(iv), (c)-(i), (d)-(iii).`,sub:"Zoology",ch:"Chemical Coordination and Integration"},{id:"neet-2019-q014",pn:14,y:"NEET 2019 \xB7 5 May",tp:"Sexual Reproduction in Flowering Plants",st:"Seed",q:"Persistent nucellus in the seed is known as",o:["Hilum","Tegmen","Chalaza","Perisperm"],c:3,s:`In some seeds (black pepper, beet) the nucellus is not fully used up and stays.
This remaining nucellus is called perisperm.`,sub:"Botany",ch:"Sexual Reproduction in Flowering Plants"},{id:"neet-2019-q015",pn:15,y:"NEET 2019 \xB7 5 May",tp:"Mineral Nutrition",st:"Mycorrhiza",q:"Pinus seed cannot germinate and establish without fungal association. This is because :",o:["it has very hard seed coat","Its seed contain inhibitors that prevent germination","its embryo is immature","it has obligate association with mycorrhizae"],c:3,s:`Pinus has an obligate association with mycorrhizal fungi.
The fungus helps the seed take up water and minerals.
Without it the seed cannot establish.`,sub:"Botany",ch:"Mineral Nutrition"},{id:"neet-2019-q016",pn:16,y:"NEET 2019 \xB7 5 May",tp:"Cell Cycle and Cell Division",st:"G0 phase",q:"Cells in G0 phase :",o:["suspend the cell cycle","terminate the cell cycle","exit the cell cycle","enter the cell cycle"],c:0,s:`Cells that do not divide leave G1 and enter G0, the quiescent stage.
They stay metabolically active.
They have suspended the cycle for the time being.`,sub:"Botany",ch:"Cell Cycle and Cell Division"},{id:"neet-2019-q017",pn:17,y:"NEET 2019 \xB7 5 May",tp:"Digestion and Absorption",st:"Digestive glands",q:`Match the following structures with their respective location in organs :
(a) Crypts of Lieberkuhn (i) Pancreas
(b) Glisson\u2019s capsule (ii) Duodenum
(c) Islets of Langerhans (iii) Small intestine
(d) Brunner\u2019s Glands (iv) Liver
Select the correct option.`,o:["(a)-(iii), (b)-(iv), (c)-(i), (d)-(ii)","(a)-(iii), (b)-(ii), (c)-(i), (d)-(iv)","(a)-(iii), (b)-(i), (c)-(ii), (d)-(iv)","(a)-(ii), (b)-(iv), (c)-(i), (d)-(iii)"],c:0,s:`Crypts of Lieberkuhn are in the small intestine.
Glisson\u2019s capsule covers the liver.
Islets of Langerhans are in the pancreas.
Brunner\u2019s glands are in the submucosa of the duodenum.`,sub:"Zoology",ch:"Digestion and Absorption"},{id:"neet-2019-q018",pn:18,y:"NEET 2019 \xB7 5 May",tp:"Anatomy of Flowering Plants",st:"Monocot leaf",q:"Grass leaves curl inward during very dry weather. Select the most appropriate reason from the following :",o:["Shrinkage of air spaces in spongy mesophyll","Tyloses in vessels","Closure of stomata","Flaccidity of bulliform cells"],c:3,s:`Bulliform cells are large thin-walled cells on the upper epidermis of grass leaves.
In dry weather they lose water and become flaccid.
The leaf then curls inward to cut down water loss.`,sub:"Botany",ch:"Anatomy of Flowering Plants"},{id:"neet-2019-q019",pn:19,y:"NEET 2019 \xB7 5 May",tp:"Biomolecules (Biology)",st:"Enzymes",q:`Consider the following statements :
(A) Coenzyme of metal ion that is tightly bound to enzyme protein is called prosthetic group.
(B) A complete catalytic active enzyme with its bound prosthetic group is called apoenzyme
Select the correct option`,o:["Both (A) and (B) are false","(A) is false but (B) is true","Both (A) and (B) are true","(A) is true but (B) is false"],c:3,s:`A coenzyme or metal ion tightly bound to the protein part is a prosthetic group, so (A) is true.
The protein part alone is the apoenzyme.
The complete active enzyme is the holoenzyme, so (B) is false.`,sub:"Botany",ch:"Biomolecules (Biology)"},{id:"neet-2019-q020",pn:20,y:"NEET 2019 \xB7 5 May",tp:"Respiration in Plants",st:"Respiratory quotient",q:"Respiratory Quotient (RQ) value of tripalmitin is :",o:["0.07","0.09","0.9","0.7"],c:3,s:`Tripalmitin is a fat.
Fats need more oxygen than the CO2 they give out.
The RQ of fats is about 0.7.`,sub:"Botany",ch:"Respiration in Plants"},{id:"neet-2019-q021",pn:21,y:"NEET 2019 \xB7 5 May",tp:"Biological Classification",st:"Viruses, viroids and prions",q:"Which of the following statements is incorrect ?",o:["Infective constituent in viruses is the protein coat","Prions consist of abnormally folded proteins","Viroids lack a protein coat","Viruses are obligate parasites"],c:0,s:`The infective part of a virus is its genetic material (DNA or RNA), not the protein coat.
So statement 1 is incorrect.`,sub:"Botany",ch:"Biological Classification"},{id:"neet-2019-q022",pn:22,y:"NEET 2019 \xB7 5 May",tp:"Anatomy of Flowering Plants",st:"Phloem",q:"Phloem in gymnosperms lacks :",o:["Companion cells only","Both sieve tubes and companion cells","Albuminous cells and sieve cells","Sieve tubes only"],c:1,s:`Gymnosperm phloem has sieve cells and albuminous cells.
It has no sieve tubes and no companion cells.`,sub:"Botany",ch:"Anatomy of Flowering Plants"},{id:"neet-2019-q023",pn:23,y:"NEET 2019 \xB7 5 May",tp:"Molecular Basis of Inheritance",st:"Mutation and reading frame",q:`Under which of the following conditions will there be no change in the reading frame of following mRNA ?
5' AACAGCGGUGCUAUU 3'`,o:["Insertion of A and G at 4th and 5th positions respectively","Deletion of GGU from 7th, 8th and 9th positions","Insertion of G at 5th positions","Deletion of G from 5th positions"],c:1,s:`Codons are read in groups of three.
Only a change by a multiple of 3 bases keeps the frame.
Option 1 adds 2 bases and option 3 adds 1 base, so the frame shifts.
Option 4 removes 1 base, so the frame shifts.
Option 2 removes GGU (bases 7-9), 3 bases, so only one codon is lost and the frame stays the same.`,sub:"Botany",ch:"Molecular Basis of Inheritance"},{id:"neet-2019-q024",pn:24,y:"NEET 2019 \xB7 5 May",tp:"Digestion and Absorption",st:"Gastric glands",q:"Identify the cells whose secretion protects the lining of gastro-intestinal tract from various enzymes.",o:["Oxyntic Cells","Duodenal Cells","Chief Cells","Goblet Cells"],c:3,s:`Goblet cells (mucous cells) secrete mucus.
Mucus coats the lining and protects it from enzymes and acid.`,sub:"Zoology",ch:"Digestion and Absorption"},{id:"neet-2019-q025",pn:25,y:"NEET 2019 \xB7 5 May",tp:"Plant Growth and Development",st:"Photoperiodism",q:"What is the site of perception of photoperiod necessary for induction of flowering in plants ?",o:["Shoot apex","Leaves","Lateral buds","Pulvinus"],c:1,s:`Leaves perceive the light and dark period.
A hormonal signal then moves from leaves to the shoot apex to start flowering.`,sub:"Botany",ch:"Plant Growth and Development"},{id:"neet-2019-q026",pn:26,y:"NEET 2019 \xB7 5 May",tp:"Body Fluids and Circulation",st:"Cardiac output",q:"What would be the heart rate of a person if the cardiac output is 5L, blood volume in the ventricles at the end of diastole is 100 mL and at the end of ventricular systole is 50 mL ?",o:["100 beats per minute","125 beats per minute","50 beats per minute","75 beats per minute"],c:0,s:`Stroke volume = 100 \u2212 50 = 50 mL.
Cardiac output = stroke volume \xD7 heart rate.
5000 mL = 50 mL \xD7 heart rate.
Heart rate = 100 beats per minute.`,sub:"Zoology",ch:"Body Fluids and Circulation"},{id:"neet-2019-q027",pn:27,y:"NEET 2019 \xB7 5 May",tp:"Breathing and Exchange of Gases",st:"Lung volumes and capacities",q:"Tidal Volume and Expiratory Reserve Volume of an athlete is 500 mL and 1000 mL respectively. What will be his Expiratory Capacity if the Residual Volume is 1200 mL ?",o:["2200 mL","2700 mL","1500 mL","1700 mL"],c:2,s:`Expiratory capacity = tidal volume + expiratory reserve volume.
= 500 + 1000 = 1500 mL.
Residual volume is not part of it.`,sub:"Zoology",ch:"Breathing and Exchange of Gases"},{id:"neet-2019-q028",pn:28,y:"NEET 2019 \xB7 5 May",tp:"Morphology of Flowering Plants",st:"Placentation",q:"Placentation, in which ovules develop on the inner wall of the ovary or in peripheral part, is :",o:["Parietal","Free central","Basal","Axile"],c:0,s:`In parietal placentation the ovules sit on the inner wall of the ovary.
The ovary is one-chambered at first.
Note: The paper prints no solution; this working is added here.`,sub:"Botany",ch:"Morphology of Flowering Plants"},{id:"neet-2019-q029",pn:29,y:"NEET 2019 \xB7 5 May",tp:"Biodiversity and Conservation",st:"Radioactive waste",q:"Which of these following methods is the most suitable for disposal of nuclear waste ?",o:["Dump the waste within rocks under deep ocean","Bury the waste within rocks deep below the Earth\u2019s surface","Shoot the waste into space","Bury the waste under Antarctic ice-cover"],c:1,s:`Nuclear waste is highly harmful and stays radioactive for a very long time.
It is stored in suitable containers and buried within rocks deep below the Earth\u2019s surface.
Note: The paper prints no solution; this working is added here.`,sub:"Botany",ch:"Biodiversity and Conservation"},{id:"neet-2019-q030",pn:30,y:"NEET 2019 \xB7 5 May",tp:"Biological Classification",st:"Kingdom Fungi",q:"Which of the following statement is incorrect ?",o:["Conidia are produced exogenously and ascospores endogenously.","Yeasts have filamentous bodies with long thread-like hyphae.","Morels and truffles are edible delicacies.","Clauiceps is a source of many alkaloids and LSD."],c:1,s:`Yeasts are single-celled and oval.
They do not form hyphae.
So statement 2 is incorrect.`,sub:"Botany",ch:"Biological Classification"},{id:"neet-2019-q031",pn:31,y:"NEET 2019 \xB7 5 May",tp:"Microbes in Human Welfare",st:"Industrial products",q:"Which one of the following equipments is essentially required for growing microbes on a large scale, for industrial production of enzymes?",o:["Industrial oven","Bioreactor","BOD incubator","Sludge digester"],c:1,s:"Microbes are grown in large vessels called bioreactors for large-scale enzyme production.",sub:"Botany",ch:"Microbes in Human Welfare"},{id:"neet-2019-q032",pn:32,y:"NEET 2019 \xB7 5 May",tp:"Microbes in Human Welfare",st:"Microbes in household and industrial products",q:`Match the following organisms with the products they produce:
(a) Lactobacillus (i) Cheese
(b) Saccharomyces cerevisiae (ii) Curd
(c) Aspergillus niger (iii) Citric Acid
(d) Acetobacter aceti (iv) Bread
(v) Acetic Acid
Select the correct option`,o:["(a)-(iii), (b)-(iv), (c)-(v), (d)-(i)","(a)-(ii), (b)-(i), (c)-(iii), (d)-(v)","(a)-(ii), (b)-(iv), (c)-(v), (d)-(iii)","(a)-(ii), (b)-(iv), (c)-(iii), (d)-(v)"],c:3,s:`Lactobacillus turns milk into curd.
Saccharomyces cerevisiae is used to make bread.
Aspergillus niger gives citric acid.
Acetobacter aceti gives acetic acid.
So (a)-(ii), (b)-(iv), (c)-(iii), (d)-(v).`,sub:"Botany",ch:"Microbes in Human Welfare"},{id:"neet-2019-q033",pn:33,y:"NEET 2019 \xB7 5 May",tp:"Microbes in Human Welfare",st:"Animal breeding",q:"Select the incorrect statement.",o:["Inbreeding selects harmful recessive genes that reduce fertility and productivity.","Inbreeding helps in accumulation of superior genes and elimination of undesirable genes.","Inbreeding increases homozygosity.","Inbreeding is essential to evolve purelines in any animal."],c:0,s:`Inbreeding increases homozygosity.
It collects superior genes and removes harmful recessive ones.
It is needed to make pure lines.
Statement 1 says the opposite, so it is incorrect.
Note: Chapter 'Animal husbandry / breeding' is not in the list; nearest chapter used.`,sub:"Zoology",ch:"Microbes in Human Welfare"},{id:"neet-2019-q034",pn:34,y:"NEET 2019 \xB7 5 May",tp:"Human Health and Disease",st:"Immunity",q:"Which of the following immune responses is responsible for rejection of kidney graft?",o:["Inflammatory immune response","Cell-mediated immune response","Auto-immune response","Humoral immune response"],c:1,s:`Graft rejection is done by T-lymphocytes.
This is the cell-mediated immune response.`,sub:"Zoology",ch:"Human Health and Disease"},{id:"neet-2019-q035",pn:35,y:"NEET 2019 \xB7 5 May",tp:"Anatomy of Flowering Plants",st:"Secondary growth",q:"Which of the statements given below is not true about formation of Annual Rings in trees?",o:["Activity of cambium depends upon variation in climate.","Annula ring are not prominent in trees of temperate region.","Annula ring is a combination of spring wood and autumn wood produced in a year.","Differential activity of cambium causes light and dark bands of tissue-early and late wood respectively"],c:1,s:`Cambium is more active in spring and less in autumn.
This makes light early wood and dark late wood, which together form one ring.
Rings are clear in temperate trees, where seasons differ a lot.
So statement 2 is not true.`,sub:"Botany",ch:"Anatomy of Flowering Plants"},{id:"neet-2019-q036",pn:36,y:"NEET 2019 \xB7 5 May",tp:"Biotechnology and its Applications",st:"Biofortified crops",q:"Which of the following is true for Golden rice?",o:["It is drought tolerant, developed using Agrobacterium vector.","It has yellow grains, because of a gene introduced from a primitive variety of rice.","It is Vitamin A enriched, with a gene from daffodil.","It is pest resistant, with a gene from Bacillus thuringiensis."],c:2,s:`Golden rice is rich in vitamin A.
Its gene for beta-carotene comes from daffodil.`,sub:"Botany",ch:"Biotechnology and its Applications"},{id:"neet-2019-q037",pn:37,y:"NEET 2019 \xB7 5 May",tp:"Principles of Inheritance and Variation",st:"Chromosomal disorders",q:"What is the genetic disorder in which an individual has an overall masculine development, gynaecomastia, and is sterile?",o:["Edward syndrome","Down\u2019s syndrome","Turner\u2019s syndrome","Klinefelter\u2019s syndrome"],c:3,s:`Klinefelter\u2019s syndrome is XXY.
The person is male-like but has breast development and is sterile.
Note: The paper prints no solution; this working is added here.`,sub:"Zoology",ch:"Principles of Inheritance and Variation"},{id:"neet-2019-q038",pn:38,y:"NEET 2019 \xB7 5 May",tp:"Sexual Reproduction in Flowering Plants",st:"Post-fertilisation events",q:"Which one of the following statements regarding post-fertilization development in flowering plants is incorrect?",o:["Central cell develop into endosperm","Ovules develop into embryo sac","Ovary develops into fruit","Zygote develops into embryo"],c:1,s:`After fertilization the central cell gives endosperm.
The zygote gives the embryo.
The ovary becomes the fruit and the ovules become seeds.
The embryo sac forms before fertilization, so statement 2 is incorrect.`,sub:"Botany",ch:"Sexual Reproduction in Flowering Plants"},{id:"neet-2019-q039",pn:39,y:"NEET 2019 \xB7 5 May",tp:"Biodiversity and Conservation",st:"Causes of biodiversity loss",q:"Which of the following is the most important cause for animals and plants being driven to extinction?",o:["Economic exploitation","Alien species invasion","Habitat loss and fragmentation","Drought and floods"],c:2,s:`Habitat loss and fragmentation is the most important cause of extinction.
Note: The paper prints no solution; this working is added here.`,sub:"Botany",ch:"Biodiversity and Conservation"},{id:"neet-2019-q040",pn:40,y:"NEET 2019 \xB7 5 May",tp:"Reproductive Health",st:"Contraception",q:"Which of the following contraceptive methods do involve a role of hormone?",o:["CuT, Pills, Emergency contraceptives","Pills, Emergency contraceptives, Barrier methods","Lactational amenorrhea, Pills, Emergency contraceptives","Barrier method, Lactational amenorrhea, Pills"],c:2,s:`Pills and emergency contraceptives contain hormones.
In lactational amenorrhea, hormones of lactation stop ovulation.
CuT and barrier methods do not use hormones.`,sub:"Zoology",ch:"Reproductive Health"},{id:"neet-2019-q041",pn:41,y:"NEET 2019 \xB7 5 May",tp:"Animal Kingdom",st:"Classification of animals",q:`Consider following features:
(a) Organ system level of organisation
(b) Bilateral symmetry
(c) True coelomates with segmentation of body
Select the correct option of animal groups which possess all the above characteristics.`,o:["Arthropoda, Mollusca and chordata","Annelida, Mollusca and chordata","Annelida, Arthropoda and chordate","Annelida, Arthropoda and Mollusca"],c:2,s:`Annelida, Arthropoda and Chordata have organ-system level, bilateral symmetry, a true coelom and a segmented body.
Mollusca are not segmented, so options with Mollusca are wrong.`,sub:"Zoology",ch:"Animal Kingdom"},{id:"neet-2019-q042",pn:42,y:"NEET 2019 \xB7 5 May",tp:"Excretory Products and their Elimination",st:"Concentration of urine",q:"Which of the following factors is responsible for the formation of concentrated urine?",o:["Secretion of erythropoietin by Juxtaglomerular complex.","Hydrostatic pressure during glomerular filtration.","Low levels of antidiuretic hormone.","Maintaining hyperosmolarity towards inner medullary interstitum in the kidneys"],c:3,s:`A high osmolarity towards the inner medulla draws water out of the filtrate.
This makes the urine concentrated.`,sub:"Zoology",ch:"Excretory Products and their Elimination"},{id:"neet-2019-q043",pn:43,y:"NEET 2019 \xB7 5 May",tp:"Animal Kingdom",st:"Characteristics of phyla",q:`Match the following organisms with their respective characteristics:
(a) Pila (i) Flame cells
(b) Bombyx (ii) Comb plates
(c) Pleurobrachia (iii) Radula
(d) Taenia (iv) Malpighian Tubules`,o:["(a)-(ii), (b)-(iv), (c)-(iii), (d)-(i)","(a)-(iii), (b)-(ii), (c)-(iv), (d)-(i)","(a)-(iii), (b)-(ii), (c)-(i), (d)-(iv)","(a)-(iii), (b)-(iv), (c)-(ii), (d)-(i)"],c:3,s:`Pila (mollusc) has a radula.
Bombyx (insect) has Malpighian tubules.
Pleurobrachia (ctenophore) has comb plates.
Taenia (flatworm) has flame cells.
So (a)-(iii), (b)-(iv), (c)-(ii), (d)-(i).`,sub:"Zoology",ch:"Animal Kingdom"},{id:"neet-2019-q044",pn:44,y:"NEET 2019 \xB7 5 May",tp:"Transport in Plants",st:"Xylem transport",q:"Xylem translocates:",o:["Water, mineral salts and some organic nitrogen only","Water, mineral salts, some organic nitrogen and hormones","Water only","Water and mineral salts only"],c:1,s:`Xylem carries water and minerals.
It also carries some organic nitrogen and hormones.
Note: Printed solution says 'water and mineral salts only', which does not match key 2; printed key kept.`,sub:"Botany",ch:"Transport in Plants"},{id:"neet-2019-q045",pn:45,y:"NEET 2019 \xB7 5 May",tp:"Transport in Plants",st:"Phloem transport",q:"What is the direction of movement of sugars in phloem?",o:["Downward","Bi-directional","Non-multidirectional","Upward"],c:1,s:`Sugars move from source to sink.
Sources and sinks can be at either end, so movement can be up or down.
So it is bi-directional.`,sub:"Botany",ch:"Transport in Plants"},{id:"neet-2019-q046",pn:46,y:"NEET 2019 \xB7 5 May",tp:"Cell Cycle and Cell Division",st:"Phases of cell cycle",q:"The correct sequence of phases of cell cycle is:",o:["S \u2192 G1 \u2192 G2 \u2192 M","G1 \u2192 S \u2192 G2 \u2192 M","M \u2192 G1 \u2192 G2 \u2192 S","G1 \u2192 G2 \u2192 S \u2192 M"],c:1,s:"The cell cycle goes G1, then S (DNA copying), then G2, then M (division).",sub:"Botany",ch:"Cell Cycle and Cell Division"},{id:"neet-2019-q047",pn:47,y:"NEET 2019 \xB7 5 May",tp:"Cell: The Unit of Life",st:"Chromosome structure",q:"The shorter and longer arms of a submetacentric chromosome are referred to as:",o:["q-arm and p-arm respectively","m-arm and n-arm respectively","s-arm and l-arm respectively","p-arm and q-arm respectively"],c:3,s:`The shorter arm is called p and the longer arm q.
So the order shorter, longer is p-arm and q-arm.`,sub:"Botany",ch:"Cell: The Unit of Life"},{id:"neet-2019-q048",pn:48,y:"NEET 2019 \xB7 5 May",tp:"Microbes in Human Welfare",st:"Biocontrol agents",q:"Which of the following can be used as a biocontrol agent in the treatment of plant disease?",o:["Anabaena","Lactobacillus","Trichoderma","Chlorella"],c:2,s:`Trichoderma is a free-living fungus of root ecosystems.
It controls several plant pathogens, so it is a biocontrol agent.`,sub:"Botany",ch:"Microbes in Human Welfare"},{id:"neet-2019-q049",pn:49,y:"NEET 2019 \xB7 5 May",tp:"Chemical Coordination and Integration",st:"Role of insulin",q:"Which of the following glucose transporters is insulin-dependent?",o:["GLUT III","GLUT IV","GLUT I","GLUT II"],c:1,s:`GLUT IV is the insulin-dependent glucose transporter.
Insulin increases its action, so more glucose enters cells.`,sub:"Zoology",ch:"Chemical Coordination and Integration"},{id:"neet-2019-q050",pn:50,y:"NEET 2019 \xB7 5 May",tp:"Molecular Basis of Inheritance",st:"Nucleotides",q:"Purines found both in DNA and RNA are:",o:["Guanine and cytosine","Cytosine and thymine","Adenine and thymine","Adenine and guanine"],c:3,s:`Purines are adenine and guanine.
Both occur in DNA and in RNA.
Cytosine and thymine are pyrimidines.`,sub:"Botany",ch:"Molecular Basis of Inheritance"},{id:"neet-2019-q051",pn:51,y:"NEET 2019 \xB7 5 May",tp:"Human Health and Disease",st:"Drugs and abuse",q:"Drug called \u2018Heroin\u2019 is synthesized by:",o:["glycosylation of morphine","nitration of morphine","methylation of morphine","acetylation of morphine"],c:3,s:`Morphine is taken from the latex of the opium poppy.
Acetylation of morphine gives heroin (diacetylmorphine).`,sub:"Zoology",ch:"Human Health and Disease"},{id:"neet-2019-q052",pn:52,y:"NEET 2019 \xB7 5 May",tp:"Locomotion and Movement",st:"Rib cage",q:"Select the correct option.",o:["Each rib is a flat thin bone and all the ribs are connected dorsally to the thoracic vertebrae and ventrally to the sternum","There are seven pairs of vertebrosternal, three pairs of vertebrochondral and two pairs of vertebral ribs","8th, 9th and 10th pairs of ribs articulate directly with the sternum","11th and 12th pairs of ribs are connected to the sternum with the help of hyaline cartilage."],c:1,s:`There are 12 pairs of ribs.
Pairs 1-7 reach the sternum directly: vertebrosternal (true ribs).
Pairs 8-10 join the 7th rib by cartilage: vertebrochondral.
Pairs 11-12 are free at the front: vertebral (floating) ribs.
So option 2 is correct and options 3 and 4 are wrong.`,sub:"Zoology",ch:"Locomotion and Movement"},{id:"neet-2019-q053",pn:53,y:"NEET 2019 \xB7 5 May",tp:"Evolution",st:"Hardy-Weinberg principle",q:"A gene locus has two alleles A, a. If the frequency of dominant allele A is 0.4, then what will be the frequency of homozygous dominant, heterozygous and homozygous recessive individuals in the population?",o:["0.16(AA); 0.48 (Aa); 0.36(aa)","0.16 (AA); 0.36(Aa); 0.48(aa)","0.36 (AA); 0.48 (Aa); 0.16 (aa)","0.16 (AA); 0.24 (Aa); 0.36 (aa)"],c:0,s:`p = 0.4, so q = 1 \u2212 0.4 = 0.6.
AA = p\xB2 = 0.16.
Aa = 2pq = 2 \xD7 0.4 \xD7 0.6 = 0.48.
aa = q\xB2 = 0.36.
The three add up to 1.
Note: The printed solution gives Aa as 0.24 (it leaves out the factor 2); the correct 2pq is 0.48, which matches the printed key.`,sub:"Zoology",ch:"Evolution"},{id:"neet-2019-q054",pn:54,y:"NEET 2019 \xB7 5 May",tp:"Cell: The Unit of Life",st:"Mitochondria",q:"Which of the following statements regarding mitochondria is incorrect ?",o:["Inner membrane is convoluted with infoldings","Mitochondrial matrix contains single circular DNA molecule and ribosomes","Outer membrane is permeable to monomers of carbohydrates, fats and proteins","Enzymes of electron transport are embedded in outer membrane."],c:3,s:`The inner membrane forms cristae and carries the electron transport enzymes.
The outer membrane does not carry them.
So statement 4 is incorrect.`,sub:"Botany",ch:"Cell: The Unit of Life"},{id:"neet-2019-q055",pn:55,y:"NEET 2019 \xB7 5 May",tp:"Evolution",st:"Mutation theory",q:"Variations caused by mutation, as proposed by Hugo de Vries, are :",o:["small and directional","small and directionless","random and directional","random and directionless"],c:3,s:`De Vries said mutations are large, sudden and random.
They have no set direction.
So they are random and directionless.`,sub:"Zoology",ch:"Evolution"},{id:"neet-2019-q056",pn:56,y:"NEET 2019 \xB7 5 May",tp:"Biotechnology: Principles and Processes",st:"Restriction enzymes",q:"Following statements describe the characteristics of the enzyme Restriction Endonuclease. Identify the incorrect statement.",o:["The enzyme cuts the sugar-phosphate backbone at specific sites on each strand","The enzyme recognizes a specific palindromic nucleotide sequence in the DNA","The enzyme cuts DNA molecule at identified position within the DNA","The enzyme binds DNA at specific sites and cuts only one of the two strands"],c:3,s:`A restriction endonuclease recognises a palindromic site.
It cuts the sugar-phosphate backbone of both strands, a little apart.
So the claim that it cuts only one strand is incorrect.`,sub:"Botany",ch:"Biotechnology: Principles and Processes"},{id:"neet-2019-q057",pn:57,y:"NEET 2019 \xB7 5 May",tp:"Neural Control and Coordination",st:"Hypothalamus",q:"Which part of the brain is responsible for thermoregulation ?",o:["Corpus callosum","Medulla oblongata","Cerebrum","Hypothalamus"],c:3,s:"The hypothalamus has centres that control body temperature, hunger and thirst.",sub:"Zoology",ch:"Neural Control and Coordination"},{id:"neet-2019-q058",pn:58,y:"NEET 2019 \xB7 5 May",tp:"Excretory Products and their Elimination",st:"Kidney functions and dialysis",q:`Use of an artificial kindney during hemodialysis may result in :
(a) Nitrogenous waste build-up in the body
(b) Non \u2013elimination of excess potassium ions
(c) Reduced absorption of calcium ions from gastro-intestinal tract
(d) Reduced RBC production
Which of the following options is the most appropriate ?`,o:["(c) and (d) are correct","(a) and (d) are correct","(a) and (b) are correct","(b) and (c) are correct"],c:0,s:`Dialysis removes urea and excess potassium, so (a) and (b) do not happen.
But an artificial kidney cannot make erythropoietin, so RBC production falls: (d).
It cannot make active vitamin D (calcitriol) either, so calcium absorption from the gut falls: (c).
So (c) and (d) are correct.`,sub:"Zoology",ch:"Excretory Products and their Elimination"},{id:"neet-2019-q059",pn:59,y:"NEET 2019 \xB7 5 May",tp:"Biotechnology and its Applications",st:"Bt toxin",q:"What triggers activation of protoxin to active Bt toxin of Bacillus thuringiensis in boll worm ?",o:["Alkaline pH of gut","Acidic pH of stomach","Body temperature","Moist surface of midgut"],c:0,s:`Bt protoxin is an inactive crystal.
The alkaline pH of the insect gut dissolves it and activates it.`,sub:"Botany",ch:"Biotechnology and its Applications"},{id:"neet-2019-q060",pn:60,y:"NEET 2019 \xB7 5 May",tp:"Biodiversity and Conservation",st:"International protocols",q:"Which of the following protocols did aim for reducing emission of chlorofluorocarbons into the atmosphere?",o:["Gothenburg Protocol","Geneva Protocol","Montreal Protocol","Kyoto Protocol"],c:2,s:`The Montreal Protocol (1987) was made to control the release of ozone-depleting substances such as CFCs.
Note: The paper prints no solution; this working is added here.`,sub:"Botany",ch:"Biodiversity and Conservation"},{id:"neet-2019-q061",pn:61,y:"NEET 2019 \xB7 5 May",tp:"Reproductive Health",st:"Sexually transmitted infections",q:"Which of the following sexually transmitted diseases is not completely curable ?",o:["Genital herpes","Chlamydiasis","Gonorrhoea","Genital warts"],c:0,s:`Chlamydiasis and gonorrhoea are bacterial and can be cured with antibiotics.
Genital herpes is viral and is not completely curable.`,sub:"Zoology",ch:"Reproductive Health"},{id:"neet-2019-q062",pn:62,y:"NEET 2019 \xB7 5 May",tp:"Ecosystem",st:"Nitrogen cycle",q:"Thiobacillus is a group of bacteria helpful in carrying out :",o:["Nitrification","Denitrification","Nitrogen fixation","Chemoautotrophic fixation"],c:1,s:`Some bacteria, such as Thiobacillus, change nitrate back to free nitrogen.
This process is denitrification.
Note: The paper prints no solution; this working is added here.`,sub:"Botany",ch:"Ecosystem"},{id:"neet-2019-q063",pn:63,y:"NEET 2019 \xB7 5 May",tp:"Principles of Inheritance and Variation",st:"Incomplete dominance",q:"In Antirrhinum (Snapdragon), a red flower was crossed with a white flower and in F1 generation, pink flowers were obtained. When pink flowers were selfed, the F2 generation showed white, red and pink flowers. Choose the incorrect statement from the following :",o:["Ratio of F2 is (Red) : (Pink) : (White) = 1/4 : 2/4 : 1/4","Law of Segregation does not apply in this experiment","This experiment does not follow the Principle of Dominance","Pink colour in F1 is due to incomplete dominance"],c:1,s:`F1 is pink, a blend of red and white, so this is incomplete dominance (4 is right).
Neither allele dominates, so the principle of dominance is not followed (3 is right).
F2 shows 1 red : 2 pink : 1 white, which is 1/4 : 2/4 : 1/4 (1 is right).
The alleles still separate in gametes, so the law of segregation does apply.
Statement 2 is incorrect.
Note: The paper prints no solution; this working is added here.`,sub:"Botany",ch:"Principles of Inheritance and Variation"},{id:"neet-2019-q064",pn:64,y:"NEET 2019 \xB7 5 May",tp:"Evolution",st:"Natural selection",q:"In a species, the weight of newborn ranges from 2 to 5 kg. 97% of the newborn with an average weight between 3 to 3.3 kg survive whereas 99% of the infants born with weights from 2 to 2.5 kg or 4.5 to 5kg die. Which type of selection process is taking place ?",o:["Disruptive Selection","Cyclical Selection","Directional Selection","Stabilizing Selection"],c:3,s:`Babies of average weight survive.
Babies at both extremes die.
Selection favours the middle value and removes both ends.
This is stabilizing selection.`,sub:"Zoology",ch:"Evolution"},{id:"neet-2019-q065",pn:65,y:"NEET 2019 \xB7 5 May",tp:"Microbes in Human Welfare",st:"Bioactive molecules",q:"Concanavalin A is :",o:["a lectin","a pigment","an alkaloid","an essential oil"],c:0,s:`Concanavalin A is a protein that binds sugars.
Such proteins are called lectins.`,sub:"Botany",ch:"Microbes in Human Welfare"},{id:"neet-2019-q066",pn:66,y:"NEET 2019 \xB7 5 May",tp:"Body Fluids and Circulation",st:"Electrocardiogram",q:`Match the Column-I with Column-II :
Column-I: (a) P-wave (b) QRS complex (c) T-wave (d) Reduction in the size of T-wave
Column-II: (i) Depolarisation of ventricles (ii) Repolarisation of ventricles (iii) Coronary ischemia (iv) Depolarisation of atria (v) Repolarisation of atria
Select the correct option.`,o:["(a)-(ii), (b)-(i), (c)-(v), (d)-(iii)","(a)-(ii), (b)-(iii), (c)-(v), (d)-(iv)","(a)-(iv), (b)-(i), (c)-(ii), (d)-(iii)","(a)-(iv), (b)-(i), (c)-(ii), (d)-(v)"],c:2,s:`P-wave is depolarisation of atria.
QRS complex is depolarisation of ventricles.
T-wave is repolarisation of ventricles.
A smaller T-wave points to coronary ischemia.
So (a)-(iv), (b)-(i), (c)-(ii), (d)-(iii).`,sub:"Zoology",ch:"Body Fluids and Circulation"},{id:"neet-2019-q067",pn:67,y:"NEET 2019 \xB7 5 May",tp:"Molecular Basis of Inheritance",st:"Lac operon",q:`Match the following genes of the Lac operon with their respective produces :
(a) i gene (i) \u03B2-galactosidase
(b) z gene (ii) Permease
(c) a gene (iii) Repressor
(d) y gene (iv) Transacetylase
Select the correct option.`,o:["(a)-(iii), (b)-(i), (c)-(iv), (d)-(ii)","(a)-(iii), (b)-(iv), (c)-(i), (d)-(ii)","(a)-(i), (b)-(iii), (c)-(ii), (d)-(iv)","(a)-(iii), (b)-(i), (c)-(ii), (d)-(iv)"],c:0,s:`The i gene codes the repressor.
The z gene codes \u03B2-galactosidase.
The y gene codes permease.
The a gene codes transacetylase.
So (a)-(iii), (b)-(i), (c)-(iv), (d)-(ii).
Note: The paper prints no solution; this working is added here.`,sub:"Botany",ch:"Molecular Basis of Inheritance"},{id:"neet-2019-q068",pn:68,y:"NEET 2019 \xB7 5 May",tp:"Cell: The Unit of Life",st:"Lysosomes",q:"Which of the following statements is not correct ?",o:["Lysosomes are membrane bound structures.","Lysosomes are formed by the process of packaging in the endoplasmic reticulum.","Lysosomes have numerous hydrolytic enzymes.","The hydrolytic enzymes of lysosomes are active under acidic pH."],c:1,s:`Lysosomes are single-membrane sacs full of hydrolytic enzymes that work at acidic pH.
They are packaged in the Golgi apparatus, not in the endoplasmic reticulum.
So statement 2 is not correct.
Note: The paper prints no solution; this working is added here.`,sub:"Botany",ch:"Cell: The Unit of Life"},{id:"neet-2019-q069",pn:69,y:"NEET 2019 \xB7 5 May",tp:"Sexual Reproduction in Flowering Plants",st:"Apomixis",q:"In some plants, the female gamete develops into embryo without fertilization. This phenomenon is known as :",o:["Syngamy","Parthenogenesis","Autogamy","Parthenocarpy"],c:1,s:`Development of an egg into an embryo without fertilization is parthenogenesis.
Parthenocarpy is fruit formation without fertilization.
Note: The paper prints no solution; this working is added here.`,sub:"Botany",ch:"Sexual Reproduction in Flowering Plants"},{id:"neet-2019-q070",pn:70,y:"NEET 2019 \xB7 5 May",tp:"Biological Classification",st:"Modes of nutrition and symbiosis",q:`Match Column-I with Column \u2013II.
Column-I: (a) Saprophyte (b) Parasite (c) Lichens (d) Mycorrhiza
Column-II: (i) Symbiotic association or fungi with plant roots (ii) Decomposition of dead organic materials (iii) Living on living plants of animals (iv) Symbiotic association of algae and fungi
Choose the correct answer from the options given below :`,o:["(a)-(ii), (b)-(i), (c)-(iii), (d)-(iv)","(a)-(ii), (b)-(iii), (c)-(iv), (d)-(i)","(a)-(i), (b)-(ii), (c)-(iii), (d)-(iv)","(a)-(iii), (b)-(ii), (c)-(i), (d)-(iv)"],c:1,s:`A saprophyte feeds on dead organic matter.
A parasite lives on a living plant or animal.
A lichen is algae living with fungi.
Mycorrhiza is fungus living with plant roots.
So (a)-(ii), (b)-(iii), (c)-(iv), (d)-(i).`,sub:"Botany",ch:"Biological Classification"},{id:"neet-2019-q071",pn:71,y:"NEET 2019 \xB7 5 May",tp:"Microbes in Human Welfare",st:"Bioactive molecules",q:"Which of the following is a commercial blood cholesterol lowering agent ?",o:["Streptokinase","Lipases","Cyclosporin A","Statin"],c:3,s:`Statins come from the yeast Monascus purpureus.
They block the enzyme that makes cholesterol, so blood cholesterol falls.
Streptokinase removes clots and cyclosporin A is an immunosuppressant.
Note: The paper prints no solution; this working is added here.`,sub:"Botany",ch:"Microbes in Human Welfare"},{id:"neet-2019-q072",pn:72,y:"NEET 2019 \xB7 5 May",tp:"Molecular Basis of Inheritance",st:"Genetic code",q:"Which of the following features of genetic code does allow bacteria to produce human insulin by recombinant DNA technology ?",o:["Genetic code is nearly universal","Genetic code is specific","Genetic code is not ambiguous","Genetic code is redundant"],c:0,s:`The same codons code for the same amino acids in nearly all organisms.
So a human gene put into bacteria is read correctly and gives human insulin.
Note: The paper prints no solution; this working is added here.`,sub:"Botany",ch:"Molecular Basis of Inheritance"},{id:"neet-2019-q073",pn:73,y:"NEET 2019 \xB7 5 May",tp:"Structural Organisation in Animals",st:"Epithelial tissue",q:"The ciliated epithelial cells are required to move particles or mucus in a specific direction. In humans, these cells are mainly present in :",o:["Eustachian tube and Salivary duct","Bronchioles and Fallopian tubes","Bile duct and Bronchioles","Fallopian tubes and Pancreatic duct"],c:1,s:`Ciliated epithelium lines the bronchioles, where it moves mucus.
It also lines the fallopian tubes, where it moves the ovum.
So the answer is bronchioles and fallopian tubes.`,sub:"Zoology",ch:"Structural Organisation in Animals"},{id:"neet-2019-q074",pn:74,y:"NEET 2019 \xB7 5 May",tp:"Respiration in Plants",st:"Glycolysis",q:"Conversion of glucose to glucose-6phosphate, the first irreversible reaction of glycolysis, is catalyzed by",o:["Enolase","Phosphofructokinase","Aldolase","Hexokinase"],c:3,s:`In glycolysis, glucose gets a phosphate from ATP and becomes glucose-6-phosphate.
The enzyme for this step is hexokinase.
Note: The paper prints no solution; this working is added here.`,sub:"Botany",ch:"Respiration in Plants"},{id:"neet-2019-q075",pn:75,y:"NEET 2019 \xB7 5 May",tp:"Biodiversity and Conservation",st:"In situ conservation",q:"Which one of the following is not a method of in situ conservation of biodiversity?",o:["Botanical Garden","Sacred Grove","Biosphere Reserve","Wildife Sanctuary"],c:0,s:`In situ means conservation in the natural habitat.
Sacred groves, biosphere reserves and sanctuaries are in situ.
A botanical garden is ex situ.
Note: The paper prints no solution; this working is added here.`,sub:"Botany",ch:"Biodiversity and Conservation"},{id:"neet-2019-q076",pn:76,y:"NEET 2019 \xB7 5 May",tp:"Cell Cycle and Cell Division",st:"Cell theory",q:"The concept of ''Omnis cellula-e cellula'' regarding cell division was first proposed by :",o:["Schleiden","Aristotle","Rudolf Virchow","Theodore Schwann"],c:2,s:`Schleiden and Schwann gave the cell theory.
Rudolf Virchow added that new cells come from pre-existing cells.
Note: The paper prints no solution; this working is added here.`,sub:"Botany",ch:"Cell Cycle and Cell Division"},{id:"neet-2019-q077",pn:77,y:"NEET 2019 \xB7 5 May",tp:"Microbes in Human Welfare",st:"Biocontrol agents",q:"Select the correct group of biocontrol agents",o:["Oscillatoria, Rhizobium, Trichoderma","Nostoc, Azospirillium, Nucleopolyhedrovirus","Bacillus thuringiensis, Tobacco mosaic virus, Aphids","Trichoderma, Baculovirus, Bacillus thuringiensis"],c:3,s:`Trichoderma is a fungus, baculovirus a virus and Bacillus thuringiensis a bacterium, and all are used as biocontrol agents.
Oscillatoria, Rhizobium, Nostoc and Azospirillum are biofertilisers.
Tobacco mosaic virus is a plant pathogen and aphids are pests.
Note: The paper prints no solution; this working is added here.`,sub:"Botany",ch:"Microbes in Human Welfare"},{id:"neet-2019-q078",pn:78,y:"NEET 2019 \xB7 5 May",tp:"Human Health and Disease",st:"Common diseases",q:"Identify the correct pair representing the causative agent of typhoid fever and the confirmatory test for typhoid.",o:["Salmonella typhi/ Anthorone test","Salmonella typhi/ Widal test","Plasmodium vivax/ UTI test","Streptococcus pneumoniae/ Widal test"],c:1,s:`Typhoid is caused by Salmonella typhi.
It is confirmed by the Widal test.`,sub:"Zoology",ch:"Human Health and Disease"},{id:"neet-2019-q079",pn:79,y:"NEET 2019 \xB7 5 May",tp:"Principles of Inheritance and Variation",st:"Sex determination",q:"Select the incorrect statement.",o:["In domesticated fowls, sex of progeny depends on the type of sperm rather than egg.","Human males have one of their sex-chromosome much shorter than the other.","Male fruit fly is heterogametic","In male grasshoppers, 50% of sperms have no sex-chromosome."],c:0,s:`In birds the female is ZW and the male ZZ.
Sex of the chick depends on the egg (Z or W), not the sperm.
So statement 1 is incorrect.
The others are right: the human Y is short, male Drosophila is XY, and male grasshopper is XO so half the sperms have no sex chromosome.
Note: The paper prints no solution; this working is added here.`,sub:"Zoology",ch:"Principles of Inheritance and Variation"},{id:"neet-2019-q080",pn:80,y:"NEET 2019 \xB7 5 May",tp:"Structural Organisation in Animals",st:"Cockroach",q:"Select the correct sequence of organs in the alimentary canal of cockroach starting from mouth :",o:["Pharynx \u2192 Oesophagus \u2192 Gizzard \u2192 Ileum \u2192 Crop \u2192 Colon \u2192 Rectum","Pharynx \u2192 Oesophagus \u2192 Ileum \u2192 Crop \u2192 Gizzard \u2192 Colon \u2192 Rectum","Pharynx \u2192 Oesophagus \u2192 Crop \u2192 Gizzard \u2192 Ileum \u2192 Colon \u2192 Rectum","Pharynx \u2192 Oesophagus \u2192 Gizzard \u2192 Crop \u2192 Ileum \u2192 Colon \u2192 Rectum"],c:2,s:`Food goes from the mouth to pharynx and oesophagus.
Then it goes to the crop (storage) and the gizzard (grinding).
Then it passes through the ileum and colon to the rectum.`,sub:"Zoology",ch:"Structural Organisation in Animals"},{id:"neet-2019-q081",pn:81,y:"NEET 2019 \xB7 5 May",tp:"Human Health and Disease",st:"Immunity",q:"Colostrum, the yellowish fluid, secreted by mother during the initial days of lactation is very essential to impart immunity to the newborn infants because it contains :",o:["Macrophages","Immunoglobulin A","Natural killer cells","Monocytes"],c:1,s:`Colostrum is rich in antibodies, mainly IgA.
These give the baby natural passive immunity.`,sub:"Zoology",ch:"Human Health and Disease"},{id:"neet-2019-q082",pn:82,y:"NEET 2019 \xB7 5 May",tp:"Sexual Reproduction in Flowering Plants",st:"Double fertilisation",q:"What is the fate of the male gametes discharged in the synergid ?",o:["One fuses with the egg, other (s) fuse (s) with synergid nucleus.","One fuses with the egg and other fuses with central cell nuclei.","One fuses with the egg, other (s) degenerate (s) in the synergid.","All fuse with the egg."],c:1,s:`The pollen tube releases two male gametes into the synergid.
One fuses with the egg (syngamy) to form the zygote.
The other fuses with the two polar nuclei of the central cell (triple fusion).
Note: The paper prints no solution; this working is added here.`,sub:"Botany",ch:"Sexual Reproduction in Flowering Plants"},{id:"neet-2019-q083",pn:83,y:"NEET 2019 \xB7 5 May",tp:"Principles of Inheritance and Variation",st:"Linkage and gene mapping",q:"What map unit (Centimorgan) is adopted in the construction of genetic maps ?",o:["A unit of distance between genes on chromosomes, representing 1% cross over.","A unit of distance between genes on chromosomes, representing 50% cross over.","A unit of distance between two expressed genes, representing 10% cross over.","A unit of distance between two expressed genes, representing 100% cross over."],c:0,s:`Distance between genes is measured by how often they recombine.
One map unit (centimorgan) means 1% crossing over.
Note: The paper prints no solution; this working is added here.`,sub:"Botany",ch:"Principles of Inheritance and Variation"},{id:"neet-2019-q084",pn:84,y:"NEET 2019 \xB7 5 May",tp:"Reproductive Health",st:"Contraception",q:"Select the horomone-releasing Intra-Uterine Devices.",o:["Progestasert, LNG-20","Lippes Loop, Multiload 375","Vaults, LNG-20","Multiload 375, Progestasert"],c:0,s:`Progestasert and LNG-20 release hormones.
Lippes Loop is non-medicated and Multiload 375 releases copper.
Vaults are not IUDs.`,sub:"Zoology",ch:"Reproductive Health"},{id:"neet-2019-q085",pn:85,y:"NEET 2019 \xB7 5 May",tp:"The Living World",st:"Binomial nomenclature",q:"Select the correctly written scientific name of Mango which was first described by Carolus Linnaeus.",o:["Mangifera indica","Mangifera Indica","Mangifera indica Car. Linn.","Mangifera Indica Linn"],c:3,s:`A scientific name has genus and species, and the author\u2019s name is written after it.
Here the author is Linnaeus, written as Linn.
Note: Printed key 4 kept; the species name 'Indica' is printed with a capital I, while the rule is a small letter (Mangifera indica Linn.). The question as printed has this problem.`,sub:"Botany",ch:"The Living World"},{id:"neet-2019-q086",pn:86,y:"NEET 2019 \xB7 5 May",tp:"Ecosystem",st:"Greenhouse effect",q:"Which of the following pairs of gases is mainly responsible for green house effect ?",o:["Nitrogen and Sulphur dioxide","Carbon dioxide and Methane","Ozone and Ammonia","Oxygen and Nitrogen"],c:1,s:`The main greenhouse gases are carbon dioxide and methane.
Nitrogen and oxygen do not trap heat.
Note: The paper prints no solution; this working is added here.`,sub:"Botany",ch:"Ecosystem"},{id:"neet-2019-q087",pn:87,y:"NEET 2019 \xB7 5 May",tp:"Principles of Inheritance and Variation",st:"Linkage and gene mapping",q:"The frequency of recombination between gene pairs on the same chromosome as a measure of the distance between genes was explained by :",o:["Alfred Sturtevant","Sutton Boveri","T.H. Morgan","Gregor J. Mendel"],c:0,s:`Morgan found linkage and recombination.
His student Alfred Sturtevant used the recombination frequency as a measure of gene distance and made the first gene map.
Note: The paper prints no solution; this working is added here.`,sub:"Botany",ch:"Principles of Inheritance and Variation"},{id:"neet-2019-q088",pn:88,y:"NEET 2019 \xB7 5 May",tp:"Neural Control and Coordination",st:"Eye",q:"Which of the following statements in correct ?",o:["Cornea is convex, transparent layer which is highly vascularised","Cornea consists of dense matrix of collagen and is the most sensitive portion of the eye.","Cornea is an external, Transparent and protective proteinacious covering of the eye-ball","Cornea consists of dense connective tissue of elastin and can repair itself."],c:1,s:`The cornea is made of a dense matrix of collagen.
It is the most sensitive part of the eye.
It has no blood vessels, so option 1 is wrong.`,sub:"Zoology",ch:"Neural Control and Coordination"},{id:"neet-2019-q089",pn:89,y:"NEET 2019 \xB7 5 May",tp:"Locomotion and Movement",st:"Disorders of muscular system",q:"Which of the following muscular disorders is inherited ?",o:["Myasthenia gravis","Botulism","Tetany","Muscular dystrophy"],c:3,s:`Myasthenia gravis is autoimmune.
Botulism is food poisoning and tetany is due to low calcium.
Muscular dystrophy is a genetic disorder.`,sub:"Zoology",ch:"Locomotion and Movement"},{id:"neet-2019-q090",pn:90,y:"NEET 2019 \xB7 5 May",tp:"Biodiversity and Conservation",st:"Solid waste management",q:"Polyblend, a fine powder of recycled modified plastic, has proved to be a good material for :",o:["Construction of roads","Making tubes and pipes","Making plastic sacks","use as a fertilizer"],c:0,s:`Polyblend is mixed with bitumen to lay roads.
Such roads last longer and use up waste plastic.
Note: The paper prints no solution; this working is added here.`,sub:"Botany",ch:"Biodiversity and Conservation"},{id:"neet-2019-q091",pn:91,y:"NEET 2019 \xB7 5 May",tp:"Oscillations",st:"Simple harmonic motion",q:"Average velocity of a particle executing SHM in one complete vibration is :",o:["A\u03C9\xB2/2","zero","A\u03C9/2","A\u03C9"],c:1,s:`Average velocity = total displacement \xF7 time.
In one complete vibration the particle returns to its start, so displacement = 0.
Average velocity = 0/T = 0.`,sub:"Physics",ch:"Oscillations"},{id:"neet-2019-q092",pn:92,y:"NEET 2019 \xB7 5 May",tp:"Ray Optics and Optical Instruments",st:"Lenses in contact",q:"Two similar thin equi-convex lenses, of focal length f each, are kept coaxially in contact with each other such that the focal length of the combination is F1. When the space between the two lenses is filled with glycerin (which has the same refractive index (\xB5 = 1.5) as that of glass) then the equivalent focal length is F2. The ratio F1 : F2 will be :",o:["2 : 3","3 : 4","2 : 1","1 : 2"],c:3,s:`Without glycerin: 1/F1 = 1/f + 1/f = 2/f, so F1 = f/2.
With glycerin, the gap between the lenses is a glycerin lens whose faces fit the two curved surfaces. It is concave on both sides and has the same \u03BC as glass, so its focal length is \u2212f.
Then 1/F2 = 1/f + 1/f \u2212 1/f = 1/f, so F2 = f.
F1 : F2 = f/2 : f = 1 : 2.`,sfig:{w:300,h:150,alt:"Two thin equi-convex lenses in contact, each of focal length f, and the same pair with the gap between them filled with glycerin (hatched).",d:[["p","M70 40 C55.3 50 55.3 120 70 130 C84.7 120 84.7 50 70 40"],["p","M84 40 C69.3 40 69.3 130 84 130 C98.7 130 98.7 40 84 40"],["p","M190 40 C175.3 50 175.3 120 190 130 C204.7 120 204.7 50 190 40"],["p","M204 40 C189.3 40 189.3 130 204 130 C218.7 130 218.7 40 204 40"],["l",192.6,42,201.4,42],["l",194.1,44,199.9,44],["l",195.1,46,198.9,46],["l",196,48,198,48],["l",196.8,50,197.2,50],["l",196.8,120,197.2,120],["l",196,122,198,122],["l",195.1,124,198.9,124],["l",194.1,126,199.9,126],["l",192.6,128,201.4,128],["t",76,30,"f   f","ms"]]},sub:"Physics",ch:"Ray Optics and Optical Instruments"},{id:"neet-2019-q093",pn:93,y:"NEET 2019 \xB7 5 May",tp:"Laws of Motion",st:"Equilibrium of forces",q:"A particle moving with velocity V is acted by three forces shown by the vector triangle PQR. The velocity of the particle will :",o:["remain constant","change according to the smallest force QR","increase","decrease"],c:0,s:`The three force vectors form a closed triangle taken head to tail.
So their vector sum is zero and the net force is zero.
With no net force there is no acceleration, so the velocity stays the same.`,fig:{w:240,h:190,alt:"Right-angled triangle PQR with the right angle at R; the three force vectors RP, PQ and QR run round the triangle head to tail.",d:[["l",40,150,40,26,"a"],["l",40,26,190,150,"a"],["l",190,150,44,150,"a"],["t",35,22,"P","e"],["t",33,167,"R","e"],["t",197,167,"Q"]]},sub:"Physics",ch:"Laws of Motion"},{id:"neet-2019-q094",pn:94,y:"NEET 2019 \xB7 5 May",tp:"Moving Charges and Magnetism",st:"Motion in a magnetic field",q:"Ionized hydrogen atoms and \u03B1-particles with same momenta enters perpendicular to a constant magnetic field, B. The ratio of their radii of their paths rH : r\u03B1 will be :",o:["4 : 1","1 : 4","2 : 1","1 : 2"],c:2,s:`Radius r = mv/(qB) = p/(qB).
Same p and same B, so r is proportional to 1/q.
rH/r\u03B1 = q\u03B1/qH = 2e/e = 2.
So rH : r\u03B1 = 2 : 1.`,sub:"Physics",ch:"Moving Charges and Magnetism"},{id:"neet-2019-q095",pn:95,y:"NEET 2019 \xB7 5 May",tp:"Work, Energy and Power",st:"Elastic collisions",q:"Body A of mass 4m moving with speed u collides with another body B of mass 2m, at rest. The collision is head on and elastic in nature. After the collision the fraction of energy lost by the colliding body A is :",o:["4/9","5/9","1/9","8/9"],c:3,s:`For an elastic head-on collision, the velocity of A after it is v1 = (4m \u2212 2m)u/(4m + 2m) = u/3.
B's velocity is v2 = 2(4m)u/(6m) = 4u/3.
Initial KE of A = \xBD(4m)u\xB2 = 2mu\xB2.
Final KE of A = \xBD(4m)(u/3)\xB2 = 2mu\xB2/9.
Fraction lost = 1 \u2212 1/9 = 8/9.`,sub:"Physics",ch:"Work, Energy and Power"},{id:"neet-2019-q096",pn:96,y:"NEET 2019 \xB7 5 May",tp:"Motion in a Plane",st:"River crossing",q:"The speed of a swimmer in still water is 20 m/s. The speed of river water is 10 m/s and is flowing due east. If he is standing on the south bank and wishes to cross the river along the shortest path, the angle at which he should make his strokes w.r.t. north is given by :",o:["60\xBA west","45\xBA west","30\xBA west","0\xBA"],c:2,s:`For the shortest path he must go straight north.
So his westward velocity component must cancel the river's eastward 10 m/s.
20 sin\u03B8 = 10, so sin\u03B8 = 1/2.
\u03B8 = 30\xBA, measured from north towards west.`,sfig:{w:300,h:170,alt:"River banks drawn as two horizontal lines with W and E left and right and N above and below; the swimmer's velocity Vm points from the bottom point up and to the west, making angle \u03B8 with the north line.",d:[["l",10,40,265,40],["l",10,130,275,130],["l",130,130,130,40],["l",130,130,78,40,"a"],["p","M130 108 A22 22 0 0 0 119 110.9"],["t",136,110,"\u03B8"],["t",130,24,"N","m"],["t",130,150,"N","m"],["t",12,89,"W"],["t",250,89,"E"],["t",92,76,"V\u2098","e"]]},sub:"Physics",ch:"Motion in a Plane"},{id:"neet-2019-q097",pn:97,y:"NEET 2019 \xB7 5 May",tp:"Current Electricity",st:"Ammeter and voltmeter",q:"In the circuits shown below, the readings of the voltmeters and the ammeters will be :",o:["V1 = V2 and i1 = i2","V2 > V1 and i1 > i2","V2 > V1 and i1 = i2","V1 = V2 and i1 > i2"],c:0,s:`In circuit 1 the voltmeter is across the 10 \u03A9 resistor.
i1 = 10 V / 10 \u03A9 = 1 A and V1 = 10 V.
In circuit 2 the voltmeter, being ideal, has infinite resistance and no current goes through it.
The current goes through the 10 \u03A9 resistor in the top branch, so i2 = 1 A. The voltmeter reads the voltage across the top resistor, V2 = 10 V.
So V1 = V2 and i1 = i2.
Note: The drawn Circuit 2 has an extra 10 \u03A9 resistor in series with the voltmeter; the printed key 1 is kept and the working above follows it.`,fig:{w:360,h:160,alt:"Two circuits, each a 10 V cell with an ammeter and a 10 \u03A9 resistor carrying current i. In circuit 1 voltmeter V1 is directly across the 10 \u03A9 resistor; in circuit 2 voltmeter V2 is in series with a second 10 \u03A9 resistor and this branch is across the top 10 \u03A9 resistor.",d:[["l",14,30,14,100],["l",14,30,64,30],["l",104,30,154,30],["p","M64 30 L67.3 30 L70.7 26 L77.3 34 L84 26 L90.7 34 L97.3 26 L100.7 30 L104 30"],["l",154,30,154,56],["l",154,74,154,100],["c",154,65,9],["t",154,69,"A\u2081","ms"],["l",14,100,80,100],["l",87,100,154,100],["l",80,91,80,109],["l",87,95,87,105,"B"],["t",84,21,"10 \u03A9","ms"],["l",132,30,146,30,"a"],["t",139,21,"i\u2081","ms"],["t",84,118,"10 V","ms"],["t",84,140,"Circuit 1","ms"],["l",46,30,46,65],["l",46,65,75,65],["l",93,65,122,65],["l",122,65,122,30],["c",84,65,9],["t",84,69,"V\u2081","ms"],["c",46,30,1.8,"F"],["c",122,30,1.8,"F"],["l",190,30,190,100],["l",190,30,240,30],["l",280,30,330,30],["p","M240 30 L243.3 30 L246.7 26 L253.3 34 L260 26 L266.7 34 L273.3 26 L276.7 30 L280 30"],["l",330,30,330,56],["l",330,74,330,100],["c",330,65,9],["t",330,69,"A\u2082","ms"],["l",190,100,256,100],["l",263,100,330,100],["l",256,91,256,109],["l",263,95,263,105,"B"],["t",260,21,"10 \u03A9","ms"],["l",308,30,322,30,"a"],["t",315,21,"i\u2082","ms"],["t",260,118,"10 V","ms"],["t",260,140,"Circuit 2","ms"],["l",222,30,222,65],["l",222,65,230,65],["p","M230 65 L232.9 65 L235.8 61 L241.7 69 L247.5 61 L253.3 69 L259.2 61 L262.1 65 L265 65"],["l",265,65,276,65],["l",294,65,302,65],["l",302,65,302,30],["c",285,65,9],["t",285,69,"V\u2082","ms"],["t",247,55,"10 \u03A9","ms"],["c",222,30,1.8,"F"],["c",302,30,1.8,"F"]]},sub:"Physics",ch:"Current Electricity"},{id:"neet-2019-q098",pn:98,y:"NEET 2019 \xB7 5 May",tp:"Electromagnetic Induction",st:"Faraday's law",q:"A 800 turn coil of effective area 0.05 m\xB2 is kept perpendicular to a magnetic field 5 \xD7 10\u207B\u2075 T. When the plane of the coil is rotated by 90\xBA around any of its coplanar axis in 0.1 s, the emf induced in the coil will be :",o:["2 \xD7 10\u207B\xB3 V","0.02 V","2 V","0.2 V"],c:1,s:`Initial flux \u03C6i = NBA, because B is perpendicular to the plane (along the normal).
After a 90\xBA turn B lies in the plane, so \u03C6f = 0.
emf = \u0394\u03C6/\u0394t = NBA/\u0394t.
= (800 \xD7 5\xD710\u207B\u2075 \xD7 0.05)/0.1 = 2\xD710\u207B\xB3/0.1 = 0.02 V.`,sfig:{w:390,h:190,alt:"Coil lying flat with B and area vector A both pointing up, flux \u03C6i = BAN; after a 90\xBA rotation the coil stands upright with B in its plane, flux \u03C6f = 0.",d:[["p","M15 117 L37 48 L140 48 L118 117 Z"],["l",62,83,62,7,"a"],["l",103,83,103,22,"a"],["t",70,24,"B"],["t",111,41,"A"],["t",37,160,"\u03C6"],["t",46,163,"i","s"],["t",52,160,"= BAN"],["p","M192 89 Q242 48 288 86","a"],["t",242,63,"90\xBA","m"],["p","M313 18 L372 40 L372 143 L313 121 Z"],["l",339,150,339,7,"a"],["t",348,26,"B"],["t",325,175,"\u03C6"],["t",334,178,"f","s"],["t",340,175,"= 0"],["p","M71 9 L80 9 M77 6 L80 9 L77 12"],["p","M112 26 L121 26 M118 23 L121 26 L118 29"],["p","M349 11 L358 11 M355 8 L358 11 L355 14"]]},sub:"Physics",ch:"Electromagnetic Induction"},{id:"neet-2019-q099",pn:99,y:"NEET 2019 \xB7 5 May",tp:"Magnetism and Matter",st:"Earth's magnetism",q:"At a point A on the earth\u2019s surface the angle of dip, \u03B4 = +25\xBA. At a point B on the earth\u2019s surface the angle of dip, \u03B4 = \u201325\xBA. We can interpret that :",o:["A is located in the northern hemisphere and B is located in the southern hemisphere","A and B are both located in the southern hemisphere","A and B are both located in the northern hemisphere","A is located in the southern hemisphere and B is located in the northern hemisphere"],c:0,s:`A positive dip means the field points downward into the Earth, which happens in the northern hemisphere.
A negative dip means the field points upward, which happens in the southern hemisphere.
So A is in the north and B in the south.`,sfig:{w:190,h:160,alt:"Earth's field resolved into BH pointing left and BV pointing down; the resultant points down-left and the dip angle \u03B4 is marked between BH and the resultant.",d:[["l",131,40,27,40,"a"],["l",131,40,131,140,"a"],["l",131,40,28,141,"a"],["l",27,40,27,140,"d"],["l",27,140,131,140,"d"],["p","M87 40 A44 44 0 0 0 100 71","a"],["t",72,64,"\u03B4"],["t",22,28,"B"],["t",29,31,"H","s"],["t",136,150,"B"],["t",143,153,"V","s"]]},sub:"Physics",ch:"Magnetism and Matter"},{id:"neet-2019-q100",pn:100,y:"NEET 2019 \xB7 5 May",tp:"Dual Nature of Radiation and Matter",st:"de Broglie wavelength",q:`An electron is accelerated through a potential difference of 10,000 V. Its de Broglie wavelength is, (nearly) :
(me = 9 \xD7 10\u207B\xB3\xB9 kg)`,o:["12.2 \xD7 10\u207B\xB9\u2074 m","12.2 nm","12.2 \xD7 10\u207B\xB9\xB3 m","12.2 \xD7 10\u207B\xB9\xB2 m"],c:3,s:`For an electron, \u03BB = 12.27/\u221AV \xC5.
V = 10,000 V, so \u221AV = 100.
\u03BB = 12.27/100 = 0.1227 \xC5 = 12.27 \xD7 10\u207B\xB2 \xC5.
1 \xC5 = 10\u207B\xB9\u2070 m, so \u03BB = 12.27 \xD7 10\u207B\xB9\xB2 m.`,sub:"Physics",ch:"Dual Nature of Radiation and Matter"},{id:"neet-2019-q101",pn:101,y:"NEET 2019 \xB7 5 May",tp:"Oscillations",st:"Amplitude of SHM",q:"The displacement of a particle executing simple harmonic motion is given by y = A\u2080 + A sin \u03C9t + B cos \u03C9t. Then the amplitude of its oscillation is given by :",o:["\u221A(A\u2080\xB2 + (A + B)\xB2)","A + B","A\u2080 + \u221A(A\xB2 + B\xB2)","\u221A(A\xB2 + B\xB2)"],c:3,s:`A\u2080 only shifts the mean position, it does not change the amplitude.
A sin \u03C9t + B cos \u03C9t can be written as R sin(\u03C9t + \u03C6).
R\xB2 = A\xB2 + B\xB2, so the amplitude is \u221A(A\xB2 + B\xB2).`,sub:"Physics",ch:"Oscillations"},{id:"neet-2019-q102",pn:102,y:"NEET 2019 \xB7 5 May",tp:"Nuclei",st:"Alpha particle",q:"\u03B1-particle consists of :",o:["2 electrons and 4 protons only","2 protons only","2 protons and 2 neutrons only","2 electrons, 2 protons and 2 neutrons"],c:2,s:`An \u03B1-particle is the nucleus of a helium atom.
It has 2 protons and 2 neutrons and no electrons.`,sub:"Physics",ch:"Nuclei"},{id:"neet-2019-q103",pn:103,y:"NEET 2019 \xB7 5 May",tp:"Electric Charges and Fields",st:"Field of a charged shell",q:"A hollow metal sphere of radius R is uniformly charged. The electric field due to the sphere at a distance r from the centre :",o:["zero as r increases for r < R, increases as r increases for r > R","decreases as r increases for r < R and for r > R","increases as r increases for r < R and for r > R","zero as r increases for r < R, decreases as r increases for r > R"],c:3,s:`Inside a hollow charged shell (r < R) the field is zero.
At the surface E = kQ/R\xB2, its maximum value.
Outside (r > R) E = kQ/r\xB2, so E falls as r increases.
So the field is zero inside and decreases with r outside.`,sfig:{w:170,h:170,alt:"A circle representing the hollow metal sphere with plus signs spread evenly round its outside.",d:[["c",85,85,42],["t",85,31,"+","ms"],["t",104.8,34.5,"+","ms"],["t",122.3,44.6,"+","ms"],["t",135.2,60,"+","ms"],["t",142.1,78.9,"+","ms"],["t",142.1,99.1,"+","ms"],["t",135.2,118,"+","ms"],["t",122.3,133.4,"+","ms"],["t",104.8,143.5,"+","ms"],["t",85,147,"+","ms"],["t",65.2,143.5,"+","ms"],["t",47.7,133.4,"+","ms"],["t",34.8,118,"+","ms"],["t",27.9,99.1,"+","ms"],["t",27.9,78.9,"+","ms"],["t",34.8,60,"+","ms"],["t",47.7,44.6,"+","ms"],["t",65.2,34.5,"+","ms"]]},sub:"Physics",ch:"Electric Charges and Fields"},{id:"neet-2019-q104",pn:104,y:"NEET 2019 \xB7 5 May",tp:"Units and Measurements",st:"Error analysis",q:"In an experiment, the percentage of error occurred in the measurement of physical quantities A, B, C and D are 1%, 2%, 3% and 4% respectively. Then the maximum percentage of error in the measurement X, where X = A\xB2B^(1/2) / (C^(1/3) D\xB3), will be :",o:["\u201310%","10%","(3/13)%","16%"],c:3,s:`Powers multiply the percentage errors and the errors add up in the maximum case.
\u0394X/X = 2(\u0394A/A) + \xBD(\u0394B/B) + \u2153(\u0394C/C) + 3(\u0394D/D).
= 2\xD71% + \xBD\xD72% + \u2153\xD73% + 3\xD74%.
= 2 + 1 + 1 + 12 = 16%.`,sub:"Physics",ch:"Units and Measurements"},{id:"neet-2019-q105",pn:105,y:"NEET 2019 \xB7 5 May",tp:"Work, Energy and Power",st:"Work by variable force",q:"A force F = 20 + 10y acts on a particle in y-direction where F is in newton and y in meter. Work done by this force to move the particle from y = 0 to y = 1 m is :",o:["25 J","20 J","30 J","5 J"],c:0,s:`Work = \u222BF dy from 0 to 1.
= [20y + 10y\xB2/2] from 0 to 1.
= 20 + 5 = 25 J.`,sub:"Physics",ch:"Work, Energy and Power"},{id:"neet-2019-q106",pn:106,y:"NEET 2019 \xB7 5 May",tp:"Thermodynamics",st:"Thermodynamic processes",q:"In which of the following processes, heat is neither absorbed nor released by a system ?",o:["isobaric","isochoric","isothermal","adiabatic"],c:3,s:"In an adiabatic process there is no exchange of heat, so Q = 0.",sub:"Physics",ch:"Thermodynamics"},{id:"neet-2019-q107",pn:107,y:"NEET 2019 \xB7 5 May",tp:"Electromagnetic Induction",st:"Eddy currents",q:"In which of the following devices, the eddy current effect is not used ?",o:["electromagnet","electric heater","induction furnace","magnetic braking in train"],a:"1,2",s:`Induction furnaces and magnetic braking use eddy currents.
An electromagnet has a soft iron core made to keep eddy currents small.
An electric heater heats by the resistance of its wire (Joule heating), not eddy currents.
So both option 1 and option 2 fit the question.
Note: Printed key is two options, 1 and 2.`,sub:"Physics",ch:"Electromagnetic Induction"},{id:"neet-2019-q108",pn:108,y:"NEET 2019 \xB7 5 May",tp:"Thermal Properties of Matter",st:"Thermal conductivity",q:"The unit of thermal conductivity is :",o:["W m K\u207B\xB9","W m\u207B\xB9 K\u207B\xB9","J m K^b 1","J m\u207B\xB9 K\u207B\xB9"],c:1,s:`Heat current H = kA\u0394\u03B8/\u2113, so k = H\u2113/(A\u0394\u03B8).
Unit = W\xB7m/(m\xB2\xB7K) = W m\u207B\xB9 K\u207B\xB9.
Note: Option 3 is garbled in the source (printed 'J m K^b 1'); written as printed.`,sub:"Physics",ch:"Thermal Properties of Matter"},{id:"neet-2019-q109",pn:109,y:"NEET 2019 \xB7 5 May",tp:"Gravitation",st:"Variation of g with depth",q:"A body weighs 200 N on the surface of the earth. How much will it weigh half way down to the centre of the earth?",o:["250 N","100 N","150 N","200 N"],c:1,s:`Inside the earth g\u2032 = g\xB7r/R.
At half the radius r = R/2, so g\u2032 = g/2.
Weight = 200/2 = 100 N.`,sub:"Physics",ch:"Gravitation"},{id:"neet-2019-q110",pn:110,y:"NEET 2019 \xB7 5 May",tp:"Electric Charges and Fields",st:"Field of line charges",q:"Two parallel infinite line charges with linear charge densities +\u03BB C/m and \u2013\u03BB C/m are placed at a distance of 2R in free space. What is the electric field mid-way between the two line charges?",o:["\u03BB/(\u03C0 \u03B5\u2080 R) N/C","\u03BB/(2\u03C0 \u03B5\u2080 R) N/C","zero","2\u03BB/(\u03C0 \u03B5\u2080 R) N/C"],c:0,s:`Field of a line charge at distance R is 2k\u03BB/R = \u03BB/(2\u03C0 \u03B5\u2080 R).
Mid-way, the +\u03BB line pushes away and the \u2212\u03BB line pulls towards itself, so both fields point the same way.
Total E = 2 \xD7 \u03BB/(2\u03C0 \u03B5\u2080 R) = \u03BB/(\u03C0 \u03B5\u2080 R).`,sfig:{w:240,h:130,alt:"Two parallel vertical line charges, \u03BB on the left and \u2212\u03BB on the right; point P midway on a horizontal dashed line, distance R from each, with E\u2081 and E\u2082 both pointing right.",d:[["l",30,30,30,115],["l",210,30,210,115],["t",30,22,"\u03BB","m"],["t",210,22,"\u2212\u03BB","m"],["l",30,75,210,75,"d"],["l",120,75,150,75,"a"],["l",150,75,180,75,"a"],["c",120,75,2.5,"F"],["t",112,64,"P","e"],["t",135,64,"E\u2081","m"],["t",165,64,"E\u2082","m"],["t",75,94,"R","m"],["t",165,94,"R","m"]]},sub:"Physics",ch:"Electric Charges and Fields"},{id:"neet-2019-q111",pn:111,y:"NEET 2019 \xB7 5 May",tp:"Laws of Motion",st:"Vertical circular motion",q:"A mass m is attached to a thin wire and whirled in a vertical circle. The wire is most likely to break when :",o:["the mass is at the lowest point","inclined at an angle of 60\xB0 from vertical","the mass is at the highest point","the wire is horizontal"],c:0,s:`At the lowest point T \u2212 mg = mv\xB2/r, so T = mg + mv\xB2/r.
The speed is also greatest there.
So the tension is maximum at the lowest point and the wire breaks there.`,sub:"Physics",ch:"Laws of Motion"},{id:"neet-2019-q112",pn:112,y:"NEET 2019 \xB7 5 May",tp:"Semiconductor Electronics",st:"Logic gates",q:"The correct Boolean operation represented by the circuit diagram drawn is :",o:["NAND","NOR","AND","OR"],c:0,s:`The two switches are in series across the LED, so the LED is shorted only when both switches are ON.
A = 0, B = 0: LED glows (1). A = 0, B = 1: glows (1). A = 1, B = 0: glows (1). A = 1, B = 1: LED off (0).
This is the truth table of NAND: Y = (AB)\u203E.`,fig:{w:280,h:240,alt:"Circuit: +6 V, resistor R, the LED (Y), another resistor R and earth in series; switch A (positions 0 and 1) joins the node above the LED and switch B joins the node below it, with the 0 contacts of A and B wired together.",d:[["c",182,13.2,2.6,"F"],["l",182,13.2,182,24.8],["p","M182 24.8 L182 26.6 L178.5 28.3 L185.5 31.9 L178.5 35.4 L185.5 38.9 L178.5 42.5 L182 44.2 L182 46"],["l",182,46,182,80],["p","M182 80 H202 V91.2 H182"],["l",182,91.2,182,134],["p","M182 134 L182 135.8 L178.5 137.7 L185.5 141.3 L178.5 145 L185.5 148.7 L178.5 152.3 L182 154.2 L182 156"],["l",182,156,182,218],["l",16,60,44.8,60],["l",16,60,16,168],["l",16,168,44.8,168],["l",68,60,182,60],["l",68,168,182,168],["c",48,60,2],["l",49.4,58.6,59.2,44],["c",48,168,2],["l",49.4,166.6,59.2,152],["l",173.2,218,190.8,218],["l",175.6,222,188.4,222],["l",177.6,226,186.4,226],["l",179.6,230,184.4,230],["t",194,18,"+6 V","s"],["t",188,40,"R","s"],["t",188,147.2,"R","s"],["t",210.8,88.8,"LED (Y)","s"],["t",40,76,"A","s"],["t",66,76,"1","s"],["t",60,36.8,"0","s"],["t",40,183.2,"B","s"],["t",66,183.2,"1","s"],["t",60,144.8,"0","s"]]},sub:"Physics",ch:"Semiconductor Electronics"},{id:"neet-2019-q113",pn:113,y:"NEET 2019 \xB7 5 May",tp:"Laws of Motion",st:"Circular motion and friction",q:"A block of mass 10 kg is in contact against the inner wall of a hollow cylindrical drum of radius 1 m. The coefficient of friction between the block and the inner wall of the cylinder is 0.1. The minimum angular velocity needed for the cylinder to keep the block stationary when the cylinder is vertical and rotating about its axis, will be : (g = 10 m/s\xB2)",o:["10 rad/s","10\u03C0 rad/s","\u221A10 rad/s","10/(2\u03C0) rad/s"],c:0,s:`The wall pushes the block inward with N = mr\u03C9\xB2.
Friction f = \u03BCN must balance weight: \u03BCmr\u03C9\xB2 = mg.
\u03C9\xB2 = g/(\u03BCr) = 10/(0.1\xD71) = 100.
\u03C9 = 10 rad/s.`,sfig:{w:215,h:230,alt:"Vertical hollow cylinder with a block on its inner wall; friction f drawn upward and weight mg drawn downward on the block.",d:[["l",27,23.4,27,202.5],["l",180,23.4,180,202.5],["p","M27 23.4 A76.5 13.5 0 1 0 180 23.4 A76.5 13.5 0 1 0 27 23.4"],["p","M27 202.5 A76.5 13.5 0 1 0 180 202.5 A76.5 13.5 0 1 0 27 202.5"],["p","M27 114.8 A69.8 13.5 0 1 0 166.6 114.8 A69.8 13.5 0 1 0 27 114.8","d"],["r",166.5,99,12.6,29.3],["l",173.3,96.8,173.3,64.4,"a"],["l",173.3,130.5,173.3,162,"a"],["t",185.4,74.3,"f","s"],["t",185.4,159.8,"mg","s"]]},sub:"Physics",ch:"Laws of Motion"},{id:"neet-2019-q114",pn:114,y:"NEET 2019 \xB7 5 May",tp:"Mechanical Properties of Fluids",st:"Torricelli's law",q:"A small hole of area of cross-section 2 mm\xB2 is present near the bottom of a fully filled open tank of height 2 m. Taking g = 10 m/s\xB2, the rate of flow of water through the open hole would be nearly :",o:["2.23 \xD7 10\u207B\u2076 m\xB3/s","6.4 \xD7 10\u207B\u2076 m\xB3/s","12.6 \xD7 10\u207B\u2076 m\xB3/s","8.9 \xD7 10\u207B\u2076 m\xB3/s"],c:2,s:`Speed of efflux v = \u221A(2gh) = \u221A(2\xD710\xD72) = \u221A40 \u2248 6.32 m/s.
Rate of flow = A v = 2\xD710\u207B\u2076 \xD7 6.32.
\u2248 12.6 \xD7 10\u207B\u2076 m\xB3/s.`,sub:"Physics",ch:"Mechanical Properties of Fluids"},{id:"neet-2019-q115",pn:115,y:"NEET 2019 \xB7 5 May",tp:"Laws of Motion",st:"Motion on an inclined plane",q:"When an object is shot from the bottom of a long smooth inclined plane kept at an angle 60\xB0 with horizontal, it can travel a distance x\u2081 along the plane. But when the inclination is decreased to 30\xB0 and the same object is shot with the same velocity, it can travel x\u2082 distance. Then x\u2081 : x\u2082 will be :",o:["1 : \u221A3","1 : 2\u221A3","1 : \u221A2","\u221A2 : 1"],c:0,s:`Deceleration along the plane is g sin\u03B8.
0 = u\xB2 \u2212 2g sin\u03B8\xB7x, so x = u\xB2/(2g sin\u03B8), so x \u221D 1/sin\u03B8.
x\u2081/x\u2082 = sin30\xB0/sin60\xB0 = (1/2)/(\u221A3/2) = 1/\u221A3.
So x\u2081 : x\u2082 = 1 : \u221A3.`,sfig:{w:230,h:170,alt:"Right-angled inclined plane with a block at the bottom of the slope moving up it with velocity u.",d:[["p","M52.5 156 L202.5 12.5 L202.5 156 Z"],["p","M52.5 154 L31 126 L55 104 L80 131 Z"],["l",61,121.5,93,92.5,"a"],["t",100,84,"u","s"]]},sub:"Physics",ch:"Laws of Motion"},{id:"neet-2019-q116",pn:116,y:"NEET 2019 \xB7 5 May",tp:"Moving Charges and Magnetism",st:"Ampere's law",q:"A cylindrical conductor of radius R is carrying a constant current. The plot of the magnitude of the magnetic field, B with the distance, d, from the centre of the conductor, is correctly represented by the figure :",o:["Graph 1","Graph 2","Graph 3","Graph 4"],c:0,s:`Inside (d < R): B = \u03BC\u2080id/(2\u03C0R\xB2), so B \u221D d, a straight line rising from zero.
Outside (d > R): B = \u03BC\u2080i/(2\u03C0d), so B \u221D 1/d, a curve falling with d.
The two values meet at d = R, where B is maximum.
Graph 1 shows this.`,fig:{w:410,h:275,alt:"Four graphs of magnetic field B against distance d, each with R marked on the d axis: (1) B rises linearly to a peak at R then falls like 1/d; (2) B rises linearly to R then stays constant; (3) B is constant up to R then falls like 1/d; (4) no field inside, and B falls like 1/d from a high value at R.",d:[["l",30,105,30,8,"a"],["l",30,105,166,105,"a"],["t",24,14,"B","e"],["t",112,118,"R","m"],["t",168,118,"d","m"],["t",4,66,"(1)"],["l",30,105,112,40],["p","M112 40 C117 68 134 89 152 89"],["l",112,40,112,105,"d"],["l",235,105,235,8,"a"],["l",235,105,371,105,"a"],["t",229,14,"B","e"],["t",317,118,"R","m"],["t",373,118,"d","m"],["t",209,66,"(2)"],["l",235,105,317,40],["l",317,40,360,40],["l",317,40,317,105,"d"],["l",30,240,30,143,"a"],["l",30,240,166,240,"a"],["t",24,149,"B","e"],["t",112,253,"R","m"],["t",168,253,"d","m"],["t",4,201,"(3)"],["l",30,175,112,175],["p","M112 175 C117 203 134 224 152 224"],["l",112,175,112,240,"d"],["l",235,240,235,143,"a"],["l",235,240,371,240,"a"],["t",229,149,"B","e"],["t",317,253,"R","m"],["t",373,253,"d","m"],["t",209,201,"(4)"],["p","M317 175 C322 203 339 224 357 224"],["l",317,175,317,240]]},sfig:{w:240,h:275,alt:"A vertical cylindrical wire with current arrows upward, and a graph of B against d: a straight line rising from the origin to a peak at the dashed line d = R, then a decaying curve.",d:[["p","M80 32 A40 10 0 1 0 160 32 A40 10 0 1 0 80 32"],["l",80,32,80,130],["l",160,32,160,130],["p","M80 130 A40 10 0 0 0 160 130"],["l",120,14,120,152,"d"],["l",94,38,94,126,"d"],["l",107,38,107,126,"d"],["l",133,38,133,126,"d"],["l",146,38,146,126,"d"],["l",94,100,94,80,"a"],["l",107,100,107,80,"a"],["l",120,100,120,80,"a"],["l",133,100,133,80,"a"],["l",146,100,146,80,"a"],["l",75,255,75,175],["l",36,255,185,255],["l",75,255,121,195],["l",121,172,121,255,"d"],["p","M121 195 C126 222 145 240 180 243"]]},sub:"Physics",ch:"Moving Charges and Magnetism"},{id:"neet-2019-q117",pn:117,y:"NEET 2019 \xB7 5 May",tp:"Mechanical Properties of Fluids",st:"Surface tension",q:"A soap bubble, having radius of 1 mm, is blown from a detergent solution having a surface tension of 2.5\xD710\u207B\xB2 N/m. The pressure inside the bubble equals at a point Z\u2080 below the free surface of water in a container. Taking g = 10 m/s\xB2, density of water = 10\xB3 kg/m\xB3, the value of Z\u2080 is :",o:["1 cm","0.5 cm","100 cm","10 cm"],c:0,s:`Excess pressure inside a soap bubble (two surfaces) = 4T/R.
= 4 \xD7 2.5\xD710\u207B\xB2 / 10\u207B\xB3 = 100 Pa.
This equals \u03C1gZ\u2080 = 10\xB3 \xD7 10 \xD7 Z\u2080 = 10\u2074 Z\u2080.
Z\u2080 = 10\u207B\xB2 m = 1 cm.`,sub:"Physics",ch:"Mechanical Properties of Fluids"},{id:"neet-2019-q118",pn:118,y:"NEET 2019 \xB7 5 May",tp:"Gravitation",st:"Gravitational potential energy",q:"The work done to raise a mass m from the surface of the earth to a height h, which is equal to the radius of the earth, is :",o:["\xBD mgR","3/2 mgR","mgR","2 mgR"],c:0,s:`Work = increase in potential energy = GMm/R \u2212 GMm/(2R).
= GMm/(2R).
Using GM = gR\xB2, this is mgR/2.`,sub:"Physics",ch:"Gravitation"},{id:"neet-2019-q119",pn:119,y:"NEET 2019 \xB7 5 May",tp:"Current Electricity",st:"Fuse",q:"Which of the following acts as a circuit protection device ?",o:["switch","fuse","conductor","inductor"],c:1,s:"A fuse melts when the current is too high and breaks the circuit, so it protects the circuit.",sub:"Physics",ch:"Current Electricity"},{id:"neet-2019-q120",pn:120,y:"NEET 2019 \xB7 5 May",tp:"Motion in a Plane",st:"Uniform circular motion",q:"Two particles A and B are moving in uniform circular motion in concentric circles of radii rA and rB with speed vA and vB respectively. Their time period of rotation is the same. The ratio of angular speed of A to that of B will be :",o:["rB : rA","1 : 1","rA : rB","vA : vB"],c:1,s:`Angular speed \u03C9 = 2\u03C0/T.
Both have the same T, so both have the same \u03C9.
The ratio is 1 : 1.`,sub:"Physics",ch:"Motion in a Plane"},{id:"neet-2019-q121",pn:121,y:"NEET 2019 \xB7 5 May",tp:"Electromagnetic Waves",st:"Displacement current",q:"A parallel plate capacitor of capacitance 20 \u03BCF is being charged by a voltage source whose potential is changing at the rate of 3 V/s. The conduction current through the connecting wires, and the displacement current through the plates of the capacitor, would be, respectively :",o:["60 \u03BCA, zero","zero, zero","zero, 60 \u03BCA","60 \u03BCA, 60 \u03BCA"],c:3,s:`Current in the wires i = C dV/dt = 20\xD710\u207B\u2076 \xD7 3 = 60 \u03BCA.
Between the plates, the displacement current equals the conduction current, so it is also 60 \u03BCA.`,sub:"Physics",ch:"Electromagnetic Waves"},{id:"neet-2019-q122",pn:122,y:"NEET 2019 \xB7 5 May",tp:"Oscillations",st:"Circular motion and SHM",q:`The radius of circle, the period of revolution, initial position and sense of revolution are indicated in the fig.
y-projection of the radius vector of rotating particle P is :`,o:["y(t) = 3 cos(3\u03C0t/2), where y in m","y(t) = 3 cos(\u03C0t/2), where y in m","y(t) = \u20133 cos 2\u03C0t, where y in m","y(t) = 4 sin(\u03C0t/2), where y in m"],c:1,s:`At t = 0 the particle is on the y-axis at y = 3 m, so y = 3 cos(\u03C9t).
\u03C9 = 2\u03C0/T = 2\u03C0/4 = \u03C0/2 rad/s.
So y(t) = 3 cos(\u03C0t/2).`,fig:{w:230,h:205,alt:"Circle of radius 3 m centred at the origin of x-y axes; P (t = 0) is at the top of the circle on the +y axis; an arrow on the circle shows clockwise revolution, T = 4 s; a double arrow along the x axis marks the 3 m radius.",d:[["c",92,130,59],["l",92,130,92,18,"a"],["l",92,130,200,130,"a"],["t",76,28,"y","e"],["t",205,134,"x"],["t",100,60,"P(t=0)","s"],["p","M129.9 84.8 A59 59 0 0 1 140.9 97","a"],["t",150,102,"T = 4 s","s"],["l",92,141,151,141,"b"],["t",121.5,158,"3 m","ms"]]},sub:"Physics",ch:"Oscillations"},{id:"neet-2019-q123",pn:123,y:"NEET 2019 \xB7 5 May",tp:"Semiconductor Electronics",st:"p-type semiconductor",q:"For a p-type semiconductor, which of the following statements is true ?",o:["Holes are the majority carriers and pentavalent atoms are the dopants","Electrons are the majority carriers and pentavalent atoms are the dopants","Electrons are the majority carriers and trivalent atoms are teh dopants","Holes are the majority carriers and trivalent atoms are the dopants"],c:3,s:`A p-type semiconductor is doped with trivalent atoms.
Each dopant creates a hole, so holes are the majority carriers.`,sub:"Physics",ch:"Semiconductor Electronics"},{id:"neet-2019-q124",pn:124,y:"NEET 2019 \xB7 5 May",tp:"Current Electricity",st:"Combination of resistors",q:"Six similar bulbs are connected as shown in the figure with a DC source of emf E and zero internal resistance. The ratio of power consumption by the bulbs when (i) all are glowing and (ii) in the situation when two from section A and one from section B are glowing, will be -",o:["1 : 2","2 : 1","4 : 9","9 : 4"],c:3,s:`Each section has three bulbs in parallel, and sections A and B are in series with the source.
All glowing: each section R/3, so Req = 2R/3 and P\u2081 = E\xB2/(2R/3) = 3E\xB2/(2R).
Two in A, one in B: section A = R/2, section B = R, so Req = 3R/2 and P\u2082 = 2E\xB2/(3R).
P\u2081/P\u2082 = (3/2)/(2/3) = 9/4.`,fig:{w:170,h:248,alt:"Six identical bulbs in two sections A and B, three bulbs each, with a cell E at the bottom of the circuit.",d:[["l",12,15,78,15,"b"],["t",45,30,"A","m"],["l",82,15,148,15,"b"],["t",115,30,"B","m"],["l",15,75,140,75,""],["l",15,125,140,125,""],["l",15,175,140,175,""],["l",15,75,15,218,""],["l",140,75,140,218,""],["l",80,75,80,175,""],["l",15,218,76,218,""],["l",83,218,140,218,""],["l",76,210,76,226,""],["l",83,214,83,222,"B"],["t",92,238,"E",""],["p","M38 75 L38 47 A3.6666666666666665 3.6666666666666665 0 0 1 45.3 47 A3.6666666666666665 3.6666666666666665 0 0 1 52.7 47 A3.6666666666666665 3.6666666666666665 0 0 1 60 47 L60 75",""],["p","M103 75 L103 47 A3.6666666666666665 3.6666666666666665 0 0 1 110.3 47 A3.6666666666666665 3.6666666666666665 0 0 1 117.7 47 A3.6666666666666665 3.6666666666666665 0 0 1 125 47 L125 75",""],["p","M38 125 L38 97 A3.6666666666666665 3.6666666666666665 0 0 1 45.3 97 A3.6666666666666665 3.6666666666666665 0 0 1 52.7 97 A3.6666666666666665 3.6666666666666665 0 0 1 60 97 L60 125",""],["p","M103 125 L103 97 A3.6666666666666665 3.6666666666666665 0 0 1 110.3 97 A3.6666666666666665 3.6666666666666665 0 0 1 117.7 97 A3.6666666666666665 3.6666666666666665 0 0 1 125 97 L125 125",""],["p","M38 175 L38 147 A3.6666666666666665 3.6666666666666665 0 0 1 45.3 147 A3.6666666666666665 3.6666666666666665 0 0 1 52.7 147 A3.6666666666666665 3.6666666666666665 0 0 1 60 147 L60 175",""],["p","M103 175 L103 147 A3.6666666666666665 3.6666666666666665 0 0 1 110.3 147 A3.6666666666666665 3.6666666666666665 0 0 1 117.7 147 A3.6666666666666665 3.6666666666666665 0 0 1 125 147 L125 175",""]]},sub:"Physics",ch:"Current Electricity"},{id:"neet-2019-q125",pn:125,y:"NEET 2019 \xB7 5 May",tp:"Kinetic Theory",st:"Temperature and kinetic energy",q:"Increase in temperature of a gas filled in a container would lead to -",o:["decrease in its pressure","decrease in intermolecular distance","increase in its mass","increase in its kinetic energy"],c:3,s:`Kinetic energy of gas molecules is proportional to the temperature.
So raising the temperature raises the kinetic energy.
In a fixed container the pressure also rises, and mass does not change.`,sub:"Physics",ch:"Kinetic Theory"},{id:"neet-2019-q126",pn:126,y:"NEET 2019 \xB7 5 May",tp:"Wave Optics",st:"Young's double slit",q:"In a double slit experiment, when light of wavelength 400 nm was used, the angular width of the first minima formed on a screen placed 1 m away, was found to be 0.2\xB0. What will be the angular width of the first minima. If the entire experimental apparatus is immersed in water ? (\u03BCwater = 4/3)",o:["0.05\xB0","0.1\xB0","0.266\xB0","0.15\xB0"],c:3,s:`Angular width \u03B8 = \u03BB/d, so \u03B8 \u221D \u03BB.
In water \u03BB\u2032 = \u03BB/\u03BC = \u03BB/(4/3) = 3\u03BB/4.
\u03B8\u2032 = (3/4) \xD7 0.2\xB0 = 0.15\xB0.`,sub:"Physics",ch:"Wave Optics"},{id:"neet-2019-q127",pn:127,y:"NEET 2019 \xB7 5 May",tp:"Atoms",st:"Energy of electron in orbit",q:"The total energy of an electron in an atom in an orbit is \u20133.4 eV. Its kinetic and potential energies are, respectively -",o:["3.4 eV, \u2013 6.8 eV","3.4 eV, 3.4 eV","\u2013 3.4 eV, \u2013 3.4 eV","\u2013 3.4 eV, \u2013 6.8 eV"],c:0,s:`For an orbiting electron, kinetic energy = \u2212(total energy) = +3.4 eV.
Potential energy = 2 \xD7 total energy = \u22126.8 eV.
Check: 3.4 + (\u22126.8) = \u22123.4 eV.`,sub:"Physics",ch:"Atoms"},{id:"neet-2019-q128",pn:128,y:"NEET 2019 \xB7 5 May",tp:"Electromagnetic Waves",st:"Visible spectrum",q:"Which colour of the light has the longest wavelength ?",o:["green","violet","red","blue"],c:2,s:"In VIBGYOR, violet has the shortest wavelength and red has the longest.",sub:"Physics",ch:"Electromagnetic Waves"},{id:"neet-2019-q129",pn:129,y:"NEET 2019 \xB7 5 May",tp:"Ray Optics and Optical Instruments",st:"Total internal reflection",q:"In total internal reflection when the angle of incidence is equal to the critical angle for the pair of media in contact, what will angle of refraction ?",o:["equal to angle of incidence","90\xB0","180\xB0","0\xB0"],c:1,s:`At the critical angle the refracted ray grazes along the boundary.
So the angle of refraction is 90\xB0.`,sub:"Physics",ch:"Ray Optics and Optical Instruments"},{id:"neet-2019-q130",pn:130,y:"NEET 2019 \xB7 5 May",tp:"System of Particles and Rotational Motion",st:"Rolling motion",q:"A disc of radius 2 m and mass 100 kg rolls on a horizontal floor. Its centre of mass has speed of 20 cm/s. How much work is needed to stop it ?",o:["2 J","1 J","3 J","30 kJ"],c:2,s:`Work needed = total kinetic energy of the rolling disc.
KE = \xBDmv\xB2(1 + K\xB2/r\xB2), and for a disc K\xB2/r\xB2 = 1/2.
= \xBD \xD7 100 \xD7 (0.2)\xB2 \xD7 (3/2).
= 2 \xD7 1.5 = 3 J.`,sub:"Physics",ch:"System of Particles and Rotational Motion"},{id:"neet-2019-q131",pn:131,y:"NEET 2019 \xB7 5 May",tp:"Mechanical Properties of Solids",st:"Elastic potential energy",q:"When a block of mass M is suspended by a long wire of length L, the length of the wire becomes (L + l). The elastic potential energy stored in the extended wire is -",o:["\xBD Mgl","\xBD MgL","Mgl","MgL"],c:0,s:`The load goes down by l, so gravity does Mgl of work.
The force grows slowly from 0 to Mg as the wire stretches, so only half of this goes into the wire.
Elastic energy U = \xBD Mgl.`,sfig:{w:140,h:250,alt:"A wire hangs from a fixed support with block M at its lower end; a downward arrow marked l shows the extension of the wire.",d:[["l",15,12,100,12,""],["l",55,12,55,128,"B"],["l",45,128,65,128,""],["l",55,128,55,145,""],["r",33,145,46,30,""],["l",33,149,37,145,""],["l",33,155,43,145,""],["l",33,161,49,145,""],["l",33,167,55,145,""],["l",33,173,61,145,""],["l",37,175,67,145,""],["l",43,175,73,145,""],["l",49,175,79,145,""],["l",55,175,79,151,""],["l",61,175,79,157,""],["l",67,175,79,163,""],["l",73,175,79,169,""],["t",13,164,"M","m"],["l",92,162,110,162,""],["l",101,162,101,238,"a"],["t",109,200,"l",""]]},sub:"Physics",ch:"Mechanical Properties of Solids"},{id:"neet-2019-q132",pn:132,y:"NEET 2019 \xB7 5 May",tp:"System of Particles and Rotational Motion",st:"Rotational dynamics",q:"A solid cylinder of mass 2 kg and radius 4 cm is rotating about its axis at the rate of 3 rpm. The torque required to stop after 2\u03C0 revolutions is-",o:["12 \xD7 10\u207B\u2074 Nm","2 \xD7 10\u2076 Nm","2 \xD7 10\u207B\u2076 Nm","2 \xD7 10\u207B\xB3 Nm"],c:2,s:`\u03C9\u2081 = 2\u03C0 \xD7 3/60 = \u03C0/10 rad/s.
Angle turned \u03B8 = 2\u03C0 revolutions = 2\u03C0 \xD7 2\u03C0 = 4\u03C0\xB2 rad.
\u03C9\u2082\xB2 = \u03C9\u2081\xB2 \u2212 2\u03B1\u03B8 = 0 gives \u03B1 = \u03C9\u2081\xB2/(2\u03B8) = (\u03C0\xB2/100)/(8\u03C0\xB2) = 1/800 rad/s\xB2.
I = \xBDmr\xB2 = \xBD \xD7 2 \xD7 (0.04)\xB2 = 1.6 \xD7 10\u207B\xB3 kg m\xB2.
\u03C4 = I\u03B1 = 1.6\xD710\u207B\xB3/800 = 2 \xD7 10\u207B\u2076 Nm.`,sub:"Physics",ch:"System of Particles and Rotational Motion"},{id:"neet-2019-q133",pn:133,y:"NEET 2019 \xB7 5 May",tp:"Electric Charges and Fields",st:"Coulomb's law",q:"Two point charges A and B, having charges +Q and \u2013Q respectively, are placed at certain distance apart and force acting between them is F. If 25 % charge of A is transferred to B, then force between the charges becomes-",o:["16F/9","4F/3","F","9F/16"],c:3,s:`Originally F = kQ\xB2/r\xB2.
25% of Q = Q/4 moves from A to B.
A now has 3Q/4. B had \u2212Q and now has \u2212Q + Q/4 = \u22123Q/4.
F\u2032 = k(3Q/4)(3Q/4)/r\xB2 = (9/16) kQ\xB2/r\xB2 = 9F/16.`,sub:"Physics",ch:"Electric Charges and Fields"},{id:"neet-2019-q134",pn:134,y:"NEET 2019 \xB7 5 May",tp:"Ray Optics and Optical Instruments",st:"Rainbow",q:"Pick the wrong answer in the context with rainbow.",o:["An observer can see a rainbow when his front is towards the sun","Rainbow is a combined effect of dispersion, refraction and reflection of sunlight","When the light rays undergo two internal reflections in a water drop, a secondary rainbow is formed","The order of colours is reversed in the secondary rainbow"],c:0,s:`A rainbow forms when sunlight is dispersed, refracted and reflected inside water drops.
The observer must have the sun behind him, so statement 1 is wrong.
Two internal reflections give a secondary rainbow with the colour order reversed.`,sub:"Physics",ch:"Ray Optics and Optical Instruments"},{id:"neet-2019-q135",pn:135,y:"NEET 2019 \xB7 5 May",tp:"Thermal Properties of Matter",st:"Thermal expansion",q:"A copper rod of 88 cm and an aluminium rod of unknown length have their increase in length independent of increase in temperature. The length of aluminium rod is (\u03B1Cu = 1.7 \xD7 10\u207B\u2075 K\u207B\xB9 and \u03B1Al = 2.2 \xD7 10\u207B\u2075 K\u207B\xB9)",o:["88 cm","68 cm","6.8 cm","113.9 cm"],c:1,s:`The increase in length is the same for both rods at any temperature change.
\u0394\u2113 = \u2113\u03B1\u0394T, so \u2113\u2081\u03B1\u2081 = \u2113\u2082\u03B1\u2082.
88 \xD7 1.7\xD710\u207B\u2075 = \u2113 \xD7 2.2\xD710\u207B\u2075.
\u2113 = 88 \xD7 1.7/2.2 = 68 cm.
Note: The printed comment says the question should read 'difference in length' instead of 'increase in length'.`,sub:"Physics",ch:"Thermal Properties of Matter"},{id:"neet-2019-q136",pn:136,y:"NEET 2019 \xB7 5 May",tp:"Thermodynamics (Chemistry)",st:"Entropy change",q:"In which case change in entropy is negative ?",o:["Sublimation of solid to gas","2H(g) \u2192 H\u2082(g)","Evaporation of water","Expansion of a gas at temperature"],c:1,s:`Sublimation, evaporation and expansion of gas all increase disorder, so \u0394S is positive.
In 2H(g) \u2192 H\u2082(g) two particles become one, so disorder falls and \u0394S is negative.
Note: Option 4 is printed as 'Expansion of a gas at temperature' (the word 'constant' is missing in the source).`,sub:"Chemistry",ch:"Thermodynamics (Chemistry)"},{id:"neet-2019-q137",pn:137,y:"NEET 2019 \xB7 5 May",tp:"Chemical Kinetics",st:"Rate of reaction",q:"For the chemical reaction N\u2082(g) + 3H\u2082(g) \u21CC 2NH\u2083(g)",o:["\u2212d[N\u2082]/dt = \xBD d[NH\u2083]/dt","3 d[H\u2082]/dt = 2 d[NH\u2083]/dt","\u2212\u2153 d[H\u2082]/dt = \u2212\xBD d[NH\u2083]/dt","\u2212d[N\u2082]/dt = 2 d[NH\u2083]/dt"],c:0,s:`Rate = \u2212d[N\u2082]/dt = \u2212\u2153 d[H\u2082]/dt = +\xBD d[NH\u2083]/dt.
So \u2212d[N\u2082]/dt = \xBD d[NH\u2083]/dt, which is option 1.
Options 3 has a wrong sign on the NH\u2083 side, and options 2 and 4 have wrong factors.`,sub:"Chemistry",ch:"Chemical Kinetics"},{id:"neet-2019-q138",pn:138,y:"NEET 2019 \xB7 5 May",tp:"Chemical Bonding and Molecular Structure",st:"Molecular orbital theory",q:"Which of the following diatomic molecular species has only \u03C0 bonds according to Molecular Orbital Theory?",o:["C\u2082","Be\u2082","O\u2082","N\u2082"],c:0,s:`C\u2082 has 12 electrons: \u03C31s\xB2 \u03C3*1s\xB2 \u03C32s\xB2 \u03C3*2s\xB2 \u03C02p\u2093\xB2 \u03C02p\u1D67\xB2.
The \u03C3 bonding and \u03C3* antibonding electrons cancel.
The only bonding electrons left are in two \u03C0 orbitals, so C\u2082 has only \u03C0 bonds.
N\u2082 has a \u03C3 bond as well, and Be\u2082 has no net bond.`,sub:"Chemistry",ch:"Chemical Bonding and Molecular Structure"},{id:"neet-2019-q139",pn:139,y:"NEET 2019 \xB7 5 May",tp:"The p-Block Elements",st:"Group 14",q:"Which of the following is incorrect statement ?",o:["GeX\u2084(X = F, Cl, Br, I) is more stable than GeX\u2082","SnF\u2084 is ionic in nature","PbF\u2084 is covalent in nature","SiCl\u2084 is easily hydrolysed"],c:2,s:`PbF\u2084 is an ionic compound, since fluoride is small and strongly electronegative.
So the statement that it is covalent is incorrect.`,sub:"Chemistry",ch:"The p-Block Elements"},{id:"neet-2019-q140",pn:140,y:"NEET 2019 \xB7 5 May",tp:"Thermodynamics (Chemistry)",st:"Work of expansion",q:"Under isothermal condition, a gas at 300 K expands from 0.1 L to 0.25 L against a constant external pressure of 2 bar. The work done by the gas is [Given that 1 L bar = 100 J]",o:["25 J","30 J","\u201330 J","5 kJ"],c:1,s:`\u0394V = 0.25 \u2212 0.1 = 0.15 L.
Work magnitude = Pext \xD7 \u0394V = 2 bar \xD7 0.15 L = 0.3 L bar = 30 J.
The question asks for work done by the gas, which is +30 J.`,sub:"Chemistry",ch:"Thermodynamics (Chemistry)"},{id:"neet-2019-q141",pn:141,y:"NEET 2019 \xB7 5 May",tp:"Alcohols, Phenols and Ethers",st:"Basicity of oxygen",q:"The compound that is most difficult to protonate is :",o:["H\u2083C\u2013O\u2013CH\u2083","Ph\u2013O\u2013H","H\u2013O\u2013H","H\u2083C\u2013O\u2013H"],c:1,s:`Protonation puts H\u207A on the lone pair of oxygen.
In phenol the oxygen lone pair is spread into the benzene ring by resonance, so it is less available.
Also protonated phenol would lose water to give an unstable phenyl cation.
So phenol is the hardest to protonate.`,sfig:{w:270,h:135,alt:"Phenol is protonated to give protonated phenol with OH\u2082\u207A, which loses water to give the phenyl cation, the least stable carbocation.",d:[["p","M44.7 90.5 L30 99 L15.3 90.5 L15.3 73.5 L30 65 L44.7 73.5 Z",""],["l",40,89.5,31.5,94.4,""],["l",18.5,86.9,18.5,77.1,""],["l",31.5,69.6,40,74.5,""],["l",30,65,30,51,""],["t",25,46,"O\u2013H",""],["l",58,82,94,82,"a"],["t",76,76,"+H\u2295","ms"],["p","M129.7 90.5 L115 99 L100.3 90.5 L100.3 73.5 L115 65 L129.7 73.5 Z",""],["l",125,89.5,116.5,94.4,""],["l",103.5,86.9,103.5,77.1,""],["l",116.5,69.6,125,74.5,""],["l",115,65,115,51,""],["t",110,46,"O\u2013H",""],["t",97,41,"\u2295","s"],["t",115,24,"H","m"],["l",115,31,115,36,""],["l",142,82,192,82,"a"],["t",167,76,"\u2013H\u2082O","ms"],["p","M229.7 90.5 L215 99 L200.3 90.5 L200.3 73.5 L215 65 L229.7 73.5 Z",""],["l",225,89.5,216.5,94.4,""],["l",203.5,86.9,203.5,77.1,""],["l",216.5,69.6,225,74.5,""],["t",215,60,"\u2295","m"],["t",168,120,"least stable C\u2295",""]]},sub:"Chemistry",ch:"Alcohols, Phenols and Ethers"},{id:"neet-2019-q142",pn:142,y:"NEET 2019 \xB7 5 May",tp:"The s-Block Elements",st:"Anomalous behaviour of beryllium",q:"Which of the following is an amphoteric hydroxide ?",o:["Mg(OH)\u2082","Be(OH)\u2082","Sr(OH)\u2082","Ca(OH)\u2082"],c:1,s:`Be(OH)\u2082 reacts with both acids and bases, so it is amphoteric.
The hydroxides of Mg, Ca and Sr are basic.`,sub:"Chemistry",ch:"The s-Block Elements"},{id:"neet-2019-q143",pn:143,y:"NEET 2019 \xB7 5 May",tp:"The p-Block Elements",st:"Oxides and structures",q:"The correct structure of tribromooctaoxide is -",o:["(1)","(2)","(3)","(4)"],c:2,s:`The formula is Br\u2083O\u2088 with three bromine atoms joined in a chain Br\u2013Br\u2013Br.
The structure with no charges has 8 oxygens, all double bonded to bromine: three on the left Br, two on the middle Br, three on the right Br.
This is option 3.
Note: Drawings (1), (2) and (4) are described only roughly; they all carry negative charges on some O, option 3 has none.`,fig:{w:470,h:182,alt:"Four drawings of a Br\u2013Br\u2013Br chain with eight oxygen atoms attached, some as =O and some as O\u207B; option 3 has all eight as =O with no charges.",d:[["t",0,50,"(1)",""],["t",62,50,"Br","m"],["t",98,50,"Br","m"],["t",134,50,"Br","m"],["l",72,46,88,46,""],["l",108,46,124,46,""],["l",52,46,40,46,""],["t",34,50,"O","m"],["t",22,42,"\u207B",""],["l",56.8,39.6,50.1,26.2,""],["l",60,38,53.3,24.6,""],["t",49,24,"O","m"],["l",58.4,53.2,51.7,66.6,""],["t",49,76,"O","m"],["t",37,68,"\u207B",""],["l",96.2,38,96.2,26,""],["l",99.8,38,99.8,26,""],["t",98,24,"O","m"],["l",99.8,54,99.8,66,""],["l",96.2,54,96.2,66,""],["t",98,76,"O","m"],["l",144,44.2,156,44.2,""],["l",144,47.8,156,47.8,""],["t",162,50,"O","m"],["l",137.6,38.8,144.3,25.4,""],["t",147,24,"O","m"],["t",152,16,"\u207B",""],["l",137.6,53.2,144.3,66.6,""],["t",147,76,"O","m"],["t",152,68,"\u207B",""],["t",260,50,"(2)",""],["t",322,50,"Br","m"],["t",358,50,"Br","m"],["t",394,50,"Br","m"],["l",332,46,348,46,""],["l",368,46,384,46,""],["l",312,47.8,300,47.8,""],["l",312,44.2,300,44.2,""],["t",294,50,"O","m"],["l",316.8,39.6,310.1,26.2,""],["l",320,38,313.3,24.6,""],["t",309,24,"O","m"],["l",320,54,313.3,67.4,""],["l",316.8,52.4,310.1,65.8,""],["t",309,76,"O","m"],["l",358,38,358,26,""],["t",358,24,"O","m"],["t",363,16,"\u207B",""],["l",358,54,358,66,""],["t",358,76,"O","m"],["t",363,68,"\u207B",""],["l",404,44.2,416,44.2,""],["l",404,47.8,416,47.8,""],["t",422,50,"O","m"],["t",427,42,"\u207B",""],["l",396,38,402.7,24.6,""],["l",399.2,39.6,405.9,26.2,""],["t",407,24,"O","m"],["l",399.2,52.4,405.9,65.8,""],["l",396,54,402.7,67.4,""],["t",407,76,"O","m"],["t",0,142,"(3)",""],["t",62,142,"Br","m"],["t",98,142,"Br","m"],["t",134,142,"Br","m"],["l",72,138,88,138,""],["l",108,138,124,138,""],["l",52,139.8,40,139.8,""],["l",52,136.2,40,136.2,""],["t",34,142,"O","m"],["l",56.8,131.6,50.1,118.2,""],["l",60,130,53.3,116.6,""],["t",49,116,"O","m"],["l",60,146,53.3,159.4,""],["l",56.8,144.4,50.1,157.8,""],["t",49,168,"O","m"],["l",96.2,130,96.2,118,""],["l",99.8,130,99.8,118,""],["t",98,116,"O","m"],["l",99.8,146,99.8,158,""],["l",96.2,146,96.2,158,""],["t",98,168,"O","m"],["l",144,136.2,156,136.2,""],["l",144,139.8,156,139.8,""],["t",162,142,"O","m"],["l",136,130,142.7,116.6,""],["l",139.2,131.6,145.9,118.2,""],["t",147,116,"O","m"],["l",139.2,144.4,145.9,157.8,""],["l",136,146,142.7,159.4,""],["t",147,168,"O","m"],["t",260,142,"(4)",""],["t",322,142,"Br","m"],["t",358,142,"Br","m"],["t",394,142,"Br","m"],["l",332,138,348,138,""],["l",368,138,384,138,""],["l",312,139.8,300,139.8,""],["l",312,136.2,300,136.2,""],["t",294,142,"O","m"],["l",316.8,131.6,310.1,118.2,""],["l",320,130,313.3,116.6,""],["t",309,116,"O","m"],["l",320,146,313.3,159.4,""],["l",316.8,144.4,310.1,157.8,""],["t",309,168,"O","m"],["l",356.2,130,356.2,118,""],["l",359.8,130,359.8,118,""],["t",358,116,"O","m"],["l",358,146,358,158,""],["t",358,168,"O","m"],["t",363,160,"\u207B",""],["l",404,136.2,416,136.2,""],["l",404,139.8,416,139.8,""],["t",422,142,"O","m"],["t",427,134,"\u207B",""],["l",396,130,402.7,116.6,""],["l",399.2,131.6,405.9,118.2,""],["t",407,116,"O","m"],["l",397.6,145.2,404.3,158.6,""],["t",407,168,"O","m"],["t",412,160,"\u207B",""]]},sub:"Chemistry",ch:"The p-Block Elements"},{id:"neet-2019-q144",pn:144,y:"NEET 2019 \xB7 5 May",tp:"Polymers",st:"Biodegradable polymers",q:"The biodegradable polymer is -",o:["nylon-6","Buna-S","nylon-6, 6","nylon 2\u2013nylon 6"],c:3,s:`Nylon-6, nylon-6,6 and Buna-S are not broken down by microbes.
Nylon 2\u2013nylon 6 is a copolymer of glycine and amino caproic acid and is biodegradable.`,sub:"Chemistry",ch:"Polymers"},{id:"neet-2019-q145",pn:145,y:"NEET 2019 \xB7 5 May",tp:"Haloalkanes and Haloarenes",st:"Electrophilic substitution",q:"Among the following, the reaction that proceeds through an electrophilic substitution, is -",o:["Benzene + Cl\u2082, UV light \u2192 hexachlorocyclohexane (C\u2086H\u2086Cl\u2086)","Benzyl alcohol (C\u2086H\u2085\u2013CH\u2082OH) + HCl, heat \u2192 benzyl chloride (C\u2086H\u2085\u2013CH\u2082Cl) + H\u2082O","Benzenediazonium chloride (C\u2086H\u2085\u2013N\u2082\u207ACl\u207B), Cu\u2082Cl\u2082 \u2192 chlorobenzene + N\u2082","Benzene + Cl\u2082, AlCl\u2083 \u2192 chlorobenzene + HCl"],c:3,s:`In option 4, AlCl\u2083 makes Cl\u207A (an electrophile) from Cl\u2082.
Cl\u207A attacks the benzene ring and replaces one H, which is electrophilic substitution.
Option 1 is radical addition, option 2 is nucleophilic substitution on the side chain, and option 3 is the Sandmeyer reaction.`,fig:{w:365,h:212,alt:"Four reactions of benzene derivatives: (1) benzene with Cl\u2082 and UV light gives hexachlorocyclohexane; (2) benzyl alcohol with HCl on heating gives benzyl chloride; (3) benzenediazonium chloride with Cu\u2082Cl\u2082 gives chlorobenzene; (4) benzene with Cl\u2082 and AlCl\u2083 gives chlorobenzene.",d:[["t",2,46,"(1)",""],["p","M63 42 L54.5 56.7 L37.5 56.7 L29 42 L37.5 27.3 L54.5 27.3 Z",""],["l",58.4,43.5,53.5,52,""],["l",38.5,52,33.6,43.5,""],["l",41.1,30.5,50.9,30.5,""],["t",70,46,"+ Cl\u2082",""],["l",112,42,186,42,"a"],["t",149,36,"UV light","ms"],["p","M256 42 L248 55.9 L232 55.9 L224 42 L232 28.1 L248 28.1 Z",""],["l",256,42,266,42,""],["t",274,46,"Cl","m"],["l",248,55.9,253,64.5,""],["t",257,75.4,"Cl","m"],["l",232,55.9,227,64.5,""],["t",223,75.4,"Cl","m"],["l",224,42,214,42,""],["t",206,46,"Cl","m"],["l",232,28.1,227,19.5,""],["t",223,16.6,"Cl","m"],["l",248,28.1,253,19.5,""],["t",257,16.6,"Cl","m"],["t",2,106,"(2)",""],["p","M63 102 L54.5 116.7 L37.5 116.7 L29 102 L37.5 87.3 L54.5 87.3 Z",""],["l",58.4,103.5,53.5,112,""],["l",38.5,112,33.6,103.5,""],["l",41.1,90.5,50.9,90.5,""],["l",63,102,71,102,""],["t",72,106,"CH\u2082OH+HCl",""],["l",164,102,224,102,"a"],["t",194,96,"heat","ms"],["p","M267 102 L258.5 116.7 L241.5 116.7 L233 102 L241.5 87.3 L258.5 87.3 Z",""],["l",262.4,103.5,257.5,112,""],["l",242.5,112,237.6,103.5,""],["l",245.1,90.5,254.9,90.5,""],["l",267,102,275,102,""],["t",276,106,"CH\u2082Cl+H\u2082O",""],["t",2,146,"(3)",""],["p","M63 142 L54.5 156.7 L37.5 156.7 L29 142 L37.5 127.3 L54.5 127.3 Z",""],["l",58.4,143.5,53.5,152,""],["l",38.5,152,33.6,143.5,""],["l",41.1,130.5,50.9,130.5,""],["l",63,142,71,142,""],["t",72,146,"N\u2082\u207ACl\u207B",""],["l",134,142,224,142,"a"],["t",179,136,"Cu\u2082Cl\u2082","ms"],["p","M267 142 L258.5 156.7 L241.5 156.7 L233 142 L241.5 127.3 L258.5 127.3 Z",""],["l",262.4,143.5,257.5,152,""],["l",242.5,152,237.6,143.5,""],["l",245.1,130.5,254.9,130.5,""],["l",267,142,275,142,""],["t",276,146,"Cl+N\u2082",""],["t",2,186,"(4)",""],["p","M63 182 L54.5 196.7 L37.5 196.7 L29 182 L37.5 167.3 L54.5 167.3 Z",""],["l",58.4,183.5,53.5,192,""],["l",38.5,192,33.6,183.5,""],["l",41.1,170.5,50.9,170.5,""],["l",63,182,71,182,""],["t",72,186,"Cl\u2082",""],["l",112,182,224,182,"a"],["t",168,176,"AlCl\u2083","ms"],["p","M267 182 L258.5 196.7 L241.5 196.7 L233 182 L241.5 167.3 L258.5 167.3 Z",""],["l",262.4,183.5,257.5,192,""],["l",242.5,192,237.6,183.5,""],["l",245.1,170.5,254.9,170.5,""],["l",267,182,275,182,""],["t",276,186,"Cl+HCl",""]]},sub:"Chemistry",ch:"Haloalkanes and Haloarenes"},{id:"neet-2019-q146",pn:146,y:"NEET 2019 \xB7 5 May",tp:"The p-Block Elements",st:"Industrial processes",q:`Match the following :
(a) Pure nitrogen (i) Chlorine
(b) Haber process (ii) Sulphuric acid
(c) Contact process (iii) Ammonia
(d) Deacon\u2019s process (iv) Sodium azide or Barium azide`,o:["(a)-(iii), (b)-(iv), (c)-(ii), (d)-(i)","(a)-(iv), (b)-(iii), (c)-(ii), (d)-(i)","(a)-(i), (b)-(ii), (c)-(iii), (d)-(iv)","(a)-(ii), (b)-(iv), (c)-(i), (d)-(iii)"],c:1,s:`Pure N\u2082 is made by heating sodium or barium azide: Ba(N\u2083)\u2082 \u2192 Ba + 3N\u2082.
Haber process makes ammonia: N\u2082 + 3H\u2082 \u21CC 2NH\u2083.
Contact process makes sulphuric acid: 2SO\u2082 + O\u2082 \u21CC 2SO\u2083.
Deacon's process makes chlorine: 4HCl + O\u2082 \u2192 2H\u2082O + 2Cl\u2082 (CuCl\u2082 catalyst).
So (a)-(iv), (b)-(iii), (c)-(ii), (d)-(i).`,sub:"Chemistry",ch:"The p-Block Elements"},{id:"neet-2019-q147",pn:147,y:"NEET 2019 \xB7 5 May",tp:"Organic Chemistry: Some Basic Principles and Techniques",st:"Sigma and pi bonds",q:"The number of sigma (\u03C3) and pi (\u03C0) bonds in pent-2-en-4-yne is -",o:["11 \u03C3 bonds and 2 \u03C0 bonds","13 \u03C3 bonds and no \u03C0 bond","10 \u03C3 bonds and 3 \u03C0 bonds","8 \u03C3 bonds and 5\u03C0 bonds"],c:2,s:`The structure is HC\u2261C\u2013CH=CH\u2013CH\u2083.
A triple bond has 1\u03C3 + 2\u03C0 and a double bond has 1\u03C3 + 1\u03C0, so \u03C0 bonds = 2 + 1 = 3.
\u03C3 bonds: C\u2261C (1), C\u2013C (1), C=C (1), C\u2013C (1) = 4 C\u2013C \u03C3 bonds, plus 6 C\u2013H bonds (1 + 1 + 1 + 3).
Total \u03C3 = 4 + 6 = 10, \u03C0 = 3.`,sub:"Chemistry",ch:"Organic Chemistry: Some Basic Principles and Techniques"},{id:"neet-2019-q148",pn:148,y:"NEET 2019 \xB7 5 May",tp:"The s-Block Elements",st:"Biological role of Mg",q:"Enzymes that utilize ATP is phosphate transfer require an alkaline earth metal (M) as the cofactor. M is -",o:["Ca","Sr","Be","Mg"],c:3,s:`Enzymes that move phosphate from ATP need magnesium as a cofactor.
So M is Mg.`,sub:"Chemistry",ch:"The s-Block Elements"},{id:"neet-2019-q149",pn:149,y:"NEET 2019 \xB7 5 May",tp:"The p-Block Elements",st:"Structure of PCl\u2085",q:"Identify the incorrect statement related to PCl\u2085 from the following -",o:["Axial P\u2013Cl bonds are longer than equatorial P\u2013Cl bonds","PCl\u2085 molecule is non-reactive","Three equatorial P\u2013Cl bonds make an angle of 120\xBA with each other","Two axial P\u2013Cl bonds make an angle of 180\xBA with each other"],c:1,s:`PCl\u2085 is trigonal bipyramidal: three equatorial P\u2013Cl bonds at 120\xBA and two axial bonds at 180\xBA.
Axial bonds are longer and weaker, so PCl\u2085 is quite reactive.
So the statement 'non-reactive' is incorrect.`,sfig:{w:130,h:112,alt:"PCl\u2085 with P at the centre: one Cl straight up and one straight down (axial) and three Cl in the equatorial plane.",d:[["t",60,60,"P","m"],["l",60,48,60,22,""],["t",60,18,"Cl","m"],["l",60,64,60,90,""],["t",60,102,"Cl","m"],["l",52.8,52.4,32,42,""],["t",24,42,"Cl","m"],["l",52.8,59.6,32,70,""],["t",24,78,"Cl","m"],["l",68,56,91,56,""],["t",100,60,"Cl","m"]]},sub:"Chemistry",ch:"The p-Block Elements"},{id:"neet-2019-q150",pn:150,y:"NEET 2019 \xB7 5 May",tp:"Chemical Kinetics",st:"First order reactions",q:"If the rate constant for a first order reaction is k, the time (t) required for the completion of 99 % of the reaction is given by -",o:["t = 4.606/k","t = 2.303/k","t = 0.693/k","t = 6.909/k"],c:0,s:`For a first order reaction t = (2.303/k) log[a/(a \u2212 x)].
For 99% completion a \u2212 x = a/100, so a/(a \u2212 x) = 100.
t = (2.303/k) \xD7 log 100 = (2.303 \xD7 2)/k = 4.606/k.`,sub:"Chemistry",ch:"Chemical Kinetics"},{id:"neet-2019-q151",pn:151,y:"NEET 2019 \xB7 5 May",tp:"Hydrocarbons",st:"Reduction of alkynes",q:`The most suitable reagent for the following conversion, is :
H\u2083C\u2013C\u2261C\u2013CH\u2083 \u2192 cis-2-butene`,o:["Zn / HCl","Hg\xB2\u207A / H\u207A, H\u2082O","Na / liquid NH\u2083","H\u2082, Pd/C, quinoline"],c:3,s:`2-Butyne must be reduced only to the alkene, with both H atoms added on the same side.
H\u2082 with Pd/C poisoned by quinoline (Lindlar-type catalyst) gives the cis-alkene.
Na in liquid NH\u2083 gives the trans-alkene, Hg\xB2\u207A/H\u207A gives a ketone, and Zn/HCl does not reduce alkynes this way.`,sfig:{w:230,h:175,alt:"2-Butyne H\u2083C\u2013C\u2261C\u2013CH\u2083 is converted by H\u2082/Pd-C with quinoline into cis-2-butene, with both CH\u2083 groups above the double bond.",d:[["t",117,14,"2-Butyne",""],["t",14,34,"H\u2083C\u2013C\u2261C\u2013CH\u2083",""],["l",56,44,56,86,"a"],["t",64,69,"H\u2082/Pd-C  quinoline",""],["t",13,118,"H\u2083C",""],["t",110,118,"CH\u2083",""],["t",76,132,"C=C","m"],["l",45,114,60,123,""],["l",92,123,107,114,""],["t",28,144,"H",""],["t",105,144,"H",""],["l",37,134,60,126,""],["l",92,126,107,134,""],["t",108,164,"Cis-2-Butene",""]]},sub:"Chemistry",ch:"Hydrocarbons"},{id:"neet-2019-q152",pn:152,y:"NEET 2019 \xB7 5 May",tp:"The d- and f-Block Elements",st:"Oxoanions of manganese",q:"The manganate and permanganate ions are tetrahedral due to -",o:["The \u03C0-bonding involves overlap of p-orbitals of oxygen with p-orbtials of manganese","The \u03C0-bonding involves overlap of d-orbitals of oxygen with d-orbitals of manganese","The \u03C0-bonding involves overlap of p-orbitals of oxygen with d-orbitals of manganese","There is no \u03C0-bonding"],c:2,s:`Oxygen has no d orbitals in its valence shell, so the second option is not possible.
In MnO\u2084\u207B and MnO\u2084\xB2\u207B the \u03C0 bonds form by overlap of filled p orbitals of oxygen with empty d orbitals of manganese (p\u03C0\u2013d\u03C0).
This gives a tetrahedral shape.`,sfig:{w:180,h:205,alt:"Tetrahedral permanganate MnO\u2084\u207B and manganate MnO\u2084\xB2\u207B ions drawn with Mn at the centre, with the p\u03C0\u2013d\u03C0 overlap labelled on the Mn=O bonds and O\u2296 on the single-bonded oxygen.",d:[["t",46,54,"Mn","m"],["t",46,22,"O","m"],["t",53,18,"p\u03C0",""],["l",47.8,28,47.8,40,""],["l",44.2,28,44.2,40,""],["t",53,40,"d\u03C0",""],["t",20,75,"O","m"],["l",37.1,57.5,26.1,65.5,""],["l",34.9,54.5,23.9,62.5,""],["t",46,83,"O","m"],["l",47.8,59,47.8,71,""],["l",44.2,59,44.2,71,""],["t",78,68,"O","m"],["t",83,60,"\u207B",""],["l",60,52,73,60,""],["t",106,55,"MnO\u2084\u207B",""],["t",58,162,"Mn","m"],["t",56,130,"O","m"],["t",63,125,"p\u03C0",""],["l",59.8,136,59.8,148,""],["l",56.2,136,56.2,148,""],["t",64,148,"d\u03C0",""],["t",42,162,"d\u03C0","e"],["t",20,173,"p\u03C0","e"],["t",28,182,"O","m"],["l",47,166.5,35,174.5,""],["l",45,163.5,33,171.5,""],["t",57,192,"O","m"],["t",63,192,"\u207B",""],["l",59.8,168,59.8,180,""],["l",56.2,168,56.2,180,""],["t",80,177,"O","m"],["t",84,169,"\u207B",""],["l",64,163,76,170,""],["t",118,176,"MnO\u2084\xB2\u207B",""]]},sub:"Chemistry",ch:"The d- and f-Block Elements"},{id:"neet-2019-q153",pn:153,y:"NEET 2019 \xB7 5 May",tp:"Electrochemistry",st:"Nernst equation and K",q:`For a cell involving one electron E\xB0cell = 0.59 V at 298 K, the equilibrium constant for the cell reaction is :
[Given that 2.303kT/F = 0.059 V at T = 298 K]`,o:["1.0 \xD7 10\xB9\u2070","1.0 \xD7 10\xB3\u2070","1.0 \xD7 10\xB2","1.0 \xD7 10\u2075"],c:0,s:`E\xB0cell = (0.059/n) log K.
0.59 = (0.059/1) log K.
log K = 10, so K = 1.0 \xD7 10\xB9\u2070.`,sub:"Chemistry",ch:"Electrochemistry"},{id:"neet-2019-q154",pn:154,y:"NEET 2019 \xB7 5 May",tp:"Equilibrium",st:"Solubility product",q:"pH of a saturated solution of Ca(OH)\u2082 is 9. The solubility product (Ksp) of Ca(OH)\u2082 is -",o:["0.125 \xD7 10\u207B\xB9\u2075","0.5 \xD7 10\u207B\xB9\u2070","0.5 \xD7 10\u207B\xB9\u2075","0.25 \xD7 10\u207B\xB9\u2070"],c:2,s:`pH = 9, so pOH = 5 and [OH\u207B] = 10\u207B\u2075 M.
Ca(OH)\u2082 \u21CC Ca\xB2\u207A + 2OH\u207B; if s is the solubility, [OH\u207B] = 2s, so s = 0.5 \xD7 10\u207B\u2075 M.
Ksp = s \xD7 (2s)\xB2 = (0.5 \xD7 10\u207B\u2075)(10\u207B\u2075)\xB2 = 0.5 \xD7 10\u207B\xB9\u2075.`,sub:"Chemistry",ch:"Equilibrium"},{id:"neet-2019-q155",pn:155,y:"NEET 2019 \xB7 5 May",tp:"Solutions",st:"Ideal solutions",q:"For an ideal solution, the correct option is -",o:["\u0394mixH = 0 at constant T and P","\u0394mixG = 0 at constant T and P","\u0394mixS = 0 at constant T and P","\u0394mixV \u2260 0 at constant T and P"],c:0,s:`In an ideal solution the A\u2013B forces equal the A\u2013A and B\u2013B forces.
So there is no heat change and no volume change: \u0394mixH = 0 and \u0394mixV = 0.
Mixing increases disorder (\u0394mixS positive), so \u0394mixG is negative.
Only option 1 is right.`,sub:"Chemistry",ch:"Solutions"},{id:"neet-2019-q156",pn:156,y:"NEET 2019 \xB7 5 May",tp:"States of Matter",st:"Compressibility factor",q:"A gas at 350 K and 15 bar has molar volume 20 percent smaller than that for an ideal gas under the same conditions. The correct option about the gas and its compressibility factor (Z) is -",o:["Z < 1 and attractive forces are dominant","Z < 1 and repulsive forces are dominant","Z > 1 and attractive forces are dominant","Z > 1 and repulsive forces are dominant"],c:0,s:`Z = Vreal/Videal = 0.8, so Z < 1.
The gas is more compressible than ideal, which means attractive forces dominate.
Z > 1 would mean repulsive forces dominate.`,sub:"Chemistry",ch:"States of Matter"},{id:"neet-2019-q157",pn:157,y:"NEET 2019 \xB7 5 May",tp:"Amines",st:"Basic strength of amines",q:"The correct order of the basic strength of methyl substituted amines in aqueous solution is -",o:["(CH\u2083)\u2083N > (CH\u2083)\u2082NH > CH\u2083NH\u2082","CH\u2083NH\u2082 > (CH\u2083)\u2082NH > (CH\u2083)\u2083N","(CH\u2083)\u2082NH > CH\u2083NH\u2082 > (CH\u2083)\u2083N","(CH\u2083)\u2083N > CH\u2083NH\u2082 > (CH\u2083)\u2082NH"],c:2,s:`In water, basic strength depends on the +I effect of CH\u2083 groups, steric hindrance and hydrogen bonding of the ammonium ion with water.
The secondary amine balances these best and is the strongest.
The tertiary amine has the least hydrogen bonding and most steric hindrance, so it is the weakest of the three.
Order: (CH\u2083)\u2082NH > CH\u2083NH\u2082 > (CH\u2083)\u2083N.`,sub:"Chemistry",ch:"Amines"},{id:"neet-2019-q158",pn:158,y:"NEET 2019 \xB7 5 May",tp:"Classification of Elements and Periodicity in Properties",st:"Ionisation enthalpy trend",q:"For the second period elements the correct increasing order of first ionization enthalpy is -",o:["Li < B < Be < C < N < O < F < Ne","Li < Be < B < C < O < N < F < Ne","Li < Be < B < C < N < O < F < Ne","Li < B < Be < C < O < N < F < Ne"],c:3,s:`Ionisation enthalpy generally rises across the period.
Be is higher than B because Be has a filled 2s subshell.
N is higher than O because N has a half-filled 2p subshell.
So the order is Li < B < Be < C < O < N < F < Ne.`,sub:"Chemistry",ch:"Classification of Elements and Periodicity in Properties"},{id:"neet-2019-q159",pn:159,y:"NEET 2019 \xB7 5 May",tp:"Surface Chemistry",st:"Colloids",q:"Which mixture of the solution will lead to the formation of negatively charged colloidal [AgI]I\u207B",o:["50 mL of 2 M AgNO\u2083 + 50 mL of 1.5 M KI","50 mL of 0.1 M Ag NO\u2083 + 50 mL of 0.1 M KI","50 mL of 1 M AgNO\u2083 + 50 mL of 1.5 M KI","50 mL of M Ag NO\u2083 + 50 mL of 2 M KI"],a:"3,4",s:`AgNO\u2083 + KI \u2192 AgI + KNO\u2083.
AgI adsorbs I\u207B ions (to give [AgI]I\u207B) only when KI is in excess.
Option 1: 100 mmol Ag\u207A against 75 mmol I\u207B, so Ag\u207A is in excess (positive sol).
Option 2: equal amounts, no excess.
Option 3: 50 mmol Ag\u207A against 75 mmol I\u207B, so KI is in excess.
Option 4: KI is 100 mmol and AgNO\u2083 is below that, so KI is in excess.
So options 3 and 4 are correct.
Note: Printed key is two options, 3 and 4. The AgNO\u2083 strength in option 4 is printed as 'M' with the number missing.`,sub:"Chemistry",ch:"Surface Chemistry"},{id:"neet-2019-q160",pn:160,y:"NEET 2019 \xB7 5 May",tp:"Electrochemistry",st:"Gibbs energy and cell potential",q:`For the cell reaction 2Fe\xB3\u207A(aq) + 2I\u207B(aq) \u2192 2Fe\xB2\u207A(aq) + I\u2082(aq), E\xB0cell = 0.24 V at 298 K. The standard Gibbs energy (\u0394rG\xB0) of the cell reaction is :
[Given that Faraday constant F = 96500 C mol\u207B\xB9]`,o:["46.32 kJ mol\u207B\xB9","23.16 kJ mol\u207B\xB9","\u201346.32 kJ mol\u207B\xB9","\u201323.16 kJ mol\u207B\xB9"],c:2,s:`Two electrons are transferred, so n = 2.
\u0394G\xB0 = \u2212nFE\xB0 = \u22122 \xD7 96500 \xD7 0.24 = \u221246320 J mol\u207B\xB9.
= \u221246.32 kJ mol\u207B\xB9.`,sub:"Chemistry",ch:"Electrochemistry"},{id:"neet-2019-q161",pn:161,y:"NEET 2019 \xB7 5 May",tp:"The p-Block Elements",st:"Group 16 hydrides",q:"Which is the correct thermal stability order for H\u2082E (E = O, S, Se, Te and Po)?",o:["H\u2082Po < H\u2082Te < H\u2082Se < H\u2082S < H\u2082O","H\u2082Se < H\u2082Te < H\u2082Po < H\u2082O < H\u2082S","H\u2082S < H\u2082O < H\u2082Se < H\u2082Te < H\u2082Po","H\u2082O < H\u2082S < H\u2082Se < H\u2082Te < H\u2082Po"],c:0,s:`Going down the group the E\u2013H bond gets longer and weaker.
So thermal stability falls from H\u2082O to H\u2082Po.
Order: H\u2082Po < H\u2082Te < H\u2082Se < H\u2082S < H\u2082O.`,sub:"Chemistry",ch:"The p-Block Elements"},{id:"neet-2019-q162",pn:162,y:"NEET 2019 \xB7 5 May",tp:"Some Basic Concepts of Chemistry",st:"Stoichiometry",q:"The number of moles of hydrogen molecules required to produce 20 moles of ammonia through Haber\u2019s process is :",o:["30","40","10","20"],c:0,s:`N\u2082 + 3H\u2082 \u2192 2NH\u2083.
3 mol H\u2082 give 2 mol NH\u2083.
For 20 mol NH\u2083: moles of H\u2082 = 20 \xD7 3/2 = 30.`,sub:"Chemistry",ch:"Some Basic Concepts of Chemistry"},{id:"neet-2019-q163",pn:163,y:"NEET 2019 \xB7 5 May",tp:"Structure of Atom",st:"Hydrogen spectrum",q:"Which of the following series of transitions in the spectrum of hydrogen atom falls in visible region?",o:["Paschen series","Brackett series","Lyman series","Balmer series"],c:3,s:`Lyman is in the ultraviolet.
Paschen and Brackett are in the infrared.
Balmer (transitions down to n = 2) is in the visible region.`,sub:"Chemistry",ch:"Structure of Atom"},{id:"neet-2019-q164",pn:164,y:"NEET 2019 \xB7 5 May",tp:"The Solid State",st:"Voids and formula",q:"A compound is formed by cation C and anion A. The anions form hexagonal close packed (hcp) lattice and the cations occupy 75% of octahedral voids. The formula of the compound is :",o:["C\u2083A\u2084","C\u2084A\u2083","C\u2082A\u2083","C\u2083A\u2082"],c:0,s:`In hcp, the number of octahedral voids equals the number of anions.
Take 4 anions: there are 4 octahedral voids.
Cations fill 75% of them: 0.75 \xD7 4 = 3.
So C : A = 3 : 4 and the formula is C\u2083A\u2084.`,sub:"Chemistry",ch:"The Solid State"},{id:"neet-2019-q165",pn:165,y:"NEET 2019 \xB7 5 May",tp:"Biomolecules (Chemistry)",st:"Amino acids",q:"The non-essential amino acid among the following is :",o:["alanine","lysine","valine","leucine"],c:0,s:`Essential amino acids cannot be made by the body, and lysine, valine and leucine are essential.
Alanine is made in the body, so it is non-essential.`,sub:"Chemistry",ch:"Biomolecules (Chemistry)"},{id:"neet-2019-q166",pn:166,y:"NEET 2019 \xB7 5 May",tp:"Hydrocarbons",st:"Ozonolysis and Markovnikov addition",q:"An alkene \u201CA\u201D on reaction with O\u2083 and Zn + H\u2082O gives propanone and ethanal in equimolar ratio. Addition of HCl to alkene \u201CA\u201D gives \u201CB\u201D as the major product. The structure of product \u201CB\u201D is :",o:["H\u2083C\u2013CH\u2082\u2013C(CH\u2083)(Cl)\u2013CH\u2083","H\u2083C\u2013CH(Cl)\u2013CH(CH\u2083)\u2013CH\u2083","Cl\u2013CH\u2082\u2013CH\u2082\u2013CH(CH\u2083)\u2013CH\u2083","H\u2083C\u2013CH\u2082\u2013CH(CH\u2082Cl)\u2013CH\u2083"],c:0,s:`Ozonolysis gives propanone (CH\u2083)\u2082C=O and ethanal CH\u2083CHO.
Join the two carbonyl carbons with a double bond: (CH\u2083)\u2082C=CH\u2013CH\u2083 is alkene A.
HCl adds by Markovnikov's rule: H goes to the carbon with more H (CH), Cl goes to the more substituted carbon.
B = H\u2083C\u2013CH\u2082\u2013C(CH\u2083)(Cl)\u2013CH\u2083, a tertiary chloride.`,fig:{w:430,h:190,alt:"Four condensed structural formulas: (1) H\u2083C\u2013CH\u2082\u2013C(CH\u2083)(Cl)\u2013CH\u2083; (2) H\u2083C\u2013CH(Cl)\u2013CH(CH\u2083)\u2013CH\u2083; (3) Cl\u2013CH\u2082\u2013CH\u2082\u2013CH(CH\u2083)\u2013CH\u2083; (4) H\u2083C\u2013CH\u2082\u2013CH(CH\u2082Cl)\u2013CH\u2083.",d:[["t",0,52,"(1)",""],["t",39,52,"H\u2083C","m"],["l",54,48,64,48,""],["t",79,52,"CH\u2082","m"],["l",94,48,104,48,""],["t",110.5,52,"C","m"],["l",117,48,127,48,""],["t",142,52,"CH\u2083","m"],["l",110.5,39,110.5,31,""],["t",110.5,26,"CH\u2083","m"],["l",110.5,57,110.5,65,""],["t",110.5,78,"Cl","m"],["t",240,52,"(2)",""],["t",281,52,"H\u2083C","m"],["l",296,48,306,48,""],["t",318,52,"CH","m"],["l",330,48,340,48,""],["t",352,52,"CH","m"],["l",318,57,318,65,""],["t",318,78,"Cl","m"],["l",352,39,352,31,""],["t",352,26,"CH\u2083","m"],["l",352,57,352,65,""],["t",352,78,"CH\u2083","m"],["t",0,154,"(3)",""],["t",32,154,"Cl","m"],["l",40,150,50,150,""],["t",65,154,"CH\u2082","m"],["l",80,150,90,150,""],["t",105,154,"CH\u2082","m"],["l",120,150,130,150,""],["t",142,154,"CH","m"],["l",142,141,142,133,""],["t",142,128,"CH\u2083","m"],["l",142,159,142,167,""],["t",142,180,"CH\u2083","m"],["t",240,154,"(4)",""],["t",281,154,"H\u2083C","m"],["l",296,150,306,150,""],["t",321,154,"CH\u2082","m"],["l",336,150,346,150,""],["t",358,154,"CH","m"],["l",370,150,380,150,""],["t",395,154,"CH\u2083","m"],["l",358,141,358,133,""],["t",358,128,"CH\u2082Cl","m"]]},sub:"Chemistry",ch:"Hydrocarbons"},{id:"neet-2019-q167",pn:167,y:"NEET 2019 \xB7 5 May",tp:"The p-Block Elements",st:"Group 14 complexes",q:"Which of the following species is not stable?",o:["[Sn(OH)\u2086]\xB2\u207B","[SiCl\u2086]\xB2\u207B","[SiF\u2086]\xB2\u207B","[GeCl\u2086]\xB2\u207B"],c:1,s:`Silicon can expand its octahedron using d orbitals.
But six large Cl atoms cannot fit around the small Si atom, which gives steric crowding.
F is small, so [SiF\u2086]\xB2\u207B is stable, and Ge and Sn are large enough for their complexes.
So [SiCl\u2086]\xB2\u207B is not stable.`,sub:"Chemistry",ch:"The p-Block Elements"},{id:"neet-2019-q168",pn:168,y:"NEET 2019 \xB7 5 May",tp:"The p-Block Elements",st:"Noble gas compounds",q:`Match the Xenon compounds in Column-I with its structure in column-II and assign the correct code :
Column-I: (a) XeF\u2084 (b) XeF\u2086 (c) XeOF\u2084 (d) XeO\u2083
Column-II: (i) pyramidal (ii) square planar (iii) distorted octahedral (iv) square pyramidal`,o:["(a)-(ii), (b)-(iii), (c)-(i), (d)-(iv)","(a)-(iii), (b)-(iv), (c)-(i), (d)-(ii)","(a)-(i), (b)-(ii), (c)-(iii), (d)-(iv)","(a)-(ii), (b)-(iii), (c)-(iv), (d)-(i)"],c:3,s:`XeF\u2084 has 4 bond pairs and 2 lone pairs: square planar.
XeF\u2086 has 6 bond pairs and 1 lone pair: distorted octahedral.
XeOF\u2084 has 5 bond pairs and 1 lone pair: square pyramidal.
XeO\u2083 has 3 bond pairs and 1 lone pair: pyramidal.
So (a)-(ii), (b)-(iii), (c)-(iv), (d)-(i).`,sfig:{w:440,h:450,alt:"Shapes of xenon compounds: XeF\u2084 square planar, XeF\u2086 distorted octahedral, XeOF\u2084 square pyramidal and XeO\u2083 pyramidal, each with its lone pair of electrons marked by dots on Xe.",d:[["t",13,61,"XeF\u2084",""],["t",124,56,"Xe","m"],["c",120,40,1,"F"],["c",128,40,1,"F"],["c",120,68,1,"F"],["c",128,68,1,"F"],["l",111.5,45.6,81,30.1,""],["t",73,30,"F","m"],["l",135.5,44,163.6,24.2,""],["t",171,23,"F","m"],["l",112.1,59.4,80.6,79.2,""],["t",73,88,"F","m"],["l",135.6,59.9,163.6,78.9,""],["t",171,88,"F","m"],["t",195,60,"Square planar",""],["t",13,219,"XeF\u2086",""],["t",124,192,"Xe","m"],["c",120,176,1,"F"],["c",128,176,1,"F"],["c",138,185,1,"F"],["c",138,193,1,"F"],["t",125,127,"F","m"],["t",73,160,"F","m"],["t",171,160,"F","m"],["t",73,224,"F","m"],["t",171,224,"F","m"],["t",125,250,"F","m"],["l",125,131,125,153,""],["l",90,153,166,153,""],["l",90,223,166,223,""],["l",125,223,125,238,""],["l",74,172,74,205,""],["l",172,172,172,205,""],["l",112.4,180.2,81,159,""],["l",135.6,180.2,167,159,""],["l",112.4,195.8,81,217,""],["l",135.6,195.8,167,217,""],["t",194,191,"Distorted octahedral",""],["t",13,331,"XeOF\u2084",""],["t",124,330,"Xe","m"],["c",120,340,1,"F"],["c",128,340,1,"F"],["t",124,289,"O","m"],["l",122.2,314,122.2,294,""],["l",125.8,314,125.8,294,""],["l",111.6,319.4,81,303.2,""],["t",73,303,"F","m"],["l",135.3,317.8,163.7,297.3,""],["t",171,296,"F","m"],["l",112,333.3,80.7,352.3,""],["t",73,361,"F","m"],["l",135.7,333.7,163.5,352,""],["t",171,361,"F","m"],["t",195,332,"Square pyramidal",""],["t",13,407,"XeO\u2083",""],["t",109,399,"Xe","m"],["c",105,382,1,"F"],["c",113,382,1,"F"],["t",73,424,"O","m"],["l",98.5,404.5,80.6,416.9,""],["l",96.5,401.5,78.5,414,""],["t",149,424,"O","m"],["l",121.8,400.9,143.2,414.2,""],["l",119.9,403.9,141.3,417.3,""],["t",110,442,"O","m"],["l",111.1,407,111.6,429,""],["l",107.5,407,108,429,""],["t",181,402,"Pyramidal",""]]},sub:"Chemistry",ch:"The p-Block Elements"},{id:"neet-2019-q169",pn:169,y:"NEET 2019 \xB7 5 May",tp:"Environmental Chemistry",st:"Greenhouse gases",q:"Among the following, the one that is not a green house gas is :",o:["ozone","sulphur dioxide","nitrous oxide","methane"],c:1,s:`Ozone, nitrous oxide and methane all trap heat and are greenhouse gases.
Sulphur dioxide is an acid rain gas, not a greenhouse gas.`,sub:"Chemistry",ch:"Environmental Chemistry"},{id:"neet-2019-q170",pn:170,y:"NEET 2019 \xB7 5 May",tp:"Redox Reactions",st:"Disproportionation",q:`Which of the following reactions are disproportionation reaction?
(a) 2Cu\u207A \u2192 Cu\xB2\u207A + Cu\u2070
(b) 3MnO\u2084\xB2\u207B + 4H\u207A \u2192 2MnO\u2084\u207B + MnO\u2082 + 2H\u2082O
(c) 2KMnO\u2084 \u2192(\u0394) K\u2082MnO\u2084 + MnO\u2082 + O\u2082
(d) 2MnO\u2084\u207B + 3Mn\xB2\u207A + 2H\u2082O \u2192 5MnO\u2082 + 4H\u207A
Select the correct option from the following :`,o:["(a), (c) and (d)","(a) and (d) only","(a) and (b) only","(a), (b) and (c)"],c:2,s:`In a disproportionation reaction one species is both oxidised and reduced.
(a) Cu\u207A goes to Cu\xB2\u207A (oxidised) and Cu\u2070 (reduced): yes.
(b) Mn(+6) goes to Mn(+7) and Mn(+4): yes.
(c) Mn(+7) is reduced and oxygen is oxidised, two different elements: no.
(d) Mn(+7) and Mn(+2) both go to Mn(+4), a comproportionation: no.
So (a) and (b) only.`,sub:"Chemistry",ch:"Redox Reactions"},{id:"neet-2019-q171",pn:171,y:"NEET 2019 \xB7 5 May",tp:"Alcohols, Phenols and Ethers",st:"Cumene to phenol",q:`The structure of intermediate A in the following reaction, is :
Cumene (isopropylbenzene) \u2192(O\u2082) A \u2192(H\u207A/H\u2082O) phenol + propanone`,o:["Ph\u2013O\u2013O\u2013CH(CH\u2083)\u2082","Ph\u2013CH(CH\u2083)\u2013CH\u2082\u2013O\u2013O\u2013H","Ph\u2013O\u2013CH(CH\u2083)\u2082 (the O drawn attached to the ring)","Ph\u2013C(CH\u2083)\u2082\u2013O\u2013O\u2013H (cumene hydroperoxide)"],c:3,s:`Air oxidation of cumene attacks the benzylic C\u2013H, which is the weakest and gives the most stable radical.
This gives cumene hydroperoxide, Ph\u2013C(CH\u2083)\u2082\u2013O\u2013O\u2013H.
Acid then splits it into phenol and propanone.
So A is option 4.`,fig:{w:440,h:320,alt:"Cumene reacts with O\u2082 to give A, and A with H\u207A and H\u2082O gives phenol and acetone; four candidate structures for A are drawn below.",d:[["p","M53 83.5 L40 91 L27 83.5 L27 68.5 L40 61 L53 68.5 Z",""],["l",48.8,82.6,41.3,86.9,""],["l",29.9,80.3,29.9,71.7,""],["l",41.3,65.1,48.8,69.4,""],["l",40,61,40,52,""],["t",40,50,"CH","m"],["l",47,44,70,34,""],["t",72,34,"CH\u2083",""],["l",47,49,70,59,""],["t",72,62,"CH\u2083",""],["l",100,76,140,76,"a"],["t",120,70,"O\u2082","ms"],["t",153,80,"A","m"],["l",166,76,208,76,"a"],["t",187,70,"H\u207A","ms"],["t",187,90,"H\u2082O","ms"],["p","M247 83.5 L234 91 L221 83.5 L221 68.5 L234 61 L247 68.5 Z",""],["l",242.8,82.6,235.3,86.9,""],["l",223.9,80.3,223.9,71.7,""],["l",235.3,65.1,242.8,69.4,""],["l",234,61,234,52,""],["t",234,50,"OH","m"],["t",256,80,"+ H\u2083C",""],["l",298,74,310,66,""],["l",310,66,322,74,""],["t",322,80,"CH\u2083",""],["l",308.2,66,308.2,52,""],["l",311.8,66,311.8,52,""],["t",310,50,"O","m"],["t",0,190,"(1)",""],["p","M53 193.5 L40 201 L27 193.5 L27 178.5 L40 171 L53 178.5 Z",""],["l",48.8,192.6,41.3,196.9,""],["l",29.9,190.3,29.9,181.7,""],["l",41.3,175.1,48.8,179.4,""],["l",40,171,40,161,""],["t",40,158,"O","m"],["l",46,154,54,154,""],["t",60,158,"O","m"],["l",66,154,74,154,""],["t",84,158,"CH","m"],["l",84,146,84,138,""],["t",84,134,"CH\u2083","m"],["l",84,162,84,170,""],["t",84,182,"CH\u2083","m"],["t",270,190,"(2)",""],["p","M323 193.5 L310 201 L297 193.5 L297 178.5 L310 171 L323 178.5 Z",""],["l",318.8,192.6,311.3,196.9,""],["l",299.9,190.3,299.9,181.7,""],["l",311.3,175.1,318.8,179.4,""],["l",310,171,310,161,""],["t",310,158,"HC","m"],["l",318,151,338,142,""],["t",340,142,"CH\u2082\u2013O\u2013O\u2013H",""],["l",318,157,338,166,""],["t",340,170,"CH\u2083",""],["t",0,300,"(3)",""],["p","M53 303.5 L40 311 L27 303.5 L27 288.5 L40 281 L53 288.5 Z",""],["l",48.8,302.6,41.3,306.9,""],["l",29.9,300.3,29.9,291.7,""],["l",41.3,285.1,48.8,289.4,""],["l",40,281,40,271,""],["t",40,268,"O","m"],["l",46,264,58,264,""],["t",68,268,"CH","m"],["l",68,256,68,248,""],["t",68,244,"CH\u2083","m"],["l",72,270,84,282,""],["t",92,292,"CH\u2083","m"],["t",270,300,"(4)",""],["p","M333 307.5 L320 315 L307 307.5 L307 292.5 L320 285 L333 292.5 Z",""],["l",328.8,306.6,321.3,310.9,""],["l",309.9,304.3,309.9,295.7,""],["l",321.3,289.1,328.8,293.4,""],["l",320,285,320,273,""],["t",320,268,"C","m"],["l",320,256,320,248,""],["t",320,244,"CH\u2083","m"],["t",287,268,"H\u2083C","m"],["l",301,264,314,264,""],["l",326,264,336,264,""],["t",342,268,"O","m"],["l",348,264,356,264,""],["t",362,268,"O","m"],["l",368,264,376,264,""],["t",382,268,"H","m"]]},sub:"Chemistry",ch:"Alcohols, Phenols and Ethers"},{id:"neet-2019-q172",pn:172,y:"NEET 2019 \xB7 5 May",tp:"Solutions",st:"Azeotropes",q:"The mixture that forms maximum boiling azeotrope is :",o:["Acetone + Carbon disulphide","Heptane + Octane","Water + Nitric acid","Ethanol + Water"],c:2,s:`Maximum boiling azeotropes come from solutions with negative deviation from Raoult's law.
Water + nitric acid shows negative deviation.
Acetone + CS\u2082 and ethanol + water show positive deviation, and heptane + octane is nearly ideal.`,sub:"Chemistry",ch:"Solutions"},{id:"neet-2019-q173",pn:173,y:"NEET 2019 \xB7 5 May",tp:"Coordination Compounds",st:"Crystal field theory",q:"What is the correct electronic configuration of the central atom in K\u2084[Fe(CN)\u2086] based on crystal field theory?",o:["e\xB3t\u2082\xB3","e\u2074t\u2082\xB2","t\u2082g\u2074 e_g\xB2","t\u2082g\u2076 e_g\u2070"],c:3,s:`The complex K\u2084[Fe(CN)\u2086] has Fe\xB2\u207A with 3d\u2076.
CN\u207B is a strong field ligand, so pairing happens in the octahedral field.
All six d electrons go in the lower t\u2082g set: t\u2082g\u2076 e_g\u2070.`,sub:"Chemistry",ch:"Coordination Compounds"},{id:"neet-2019-q174",pn:174,y:"NEET 2019 \xB7 5 May",tp:"Equilibrium",st:"Br\xF8nsted acids and bases",q:"Conjugate base for Bronsted acids H\u2082O and HF are :",o:["OH\u207B and F\u207B, respectively","H\u2083O\u207A and H\u2082F\u207A, respectively","OH\u207B and H\u2082F\u207A, respectively","H\u2083O\u207A and F\u207B, respectively"],c:0,s:`A conjugate base is formed when the acid loses H\u207A.
H\u2082O \u2212 H\u207A = OH\u207B.
HF \u2212 H\u207A = F\u207B.`,sub:"Chemistry",ch:"Equilibrium"},{id:"neet-2019-q175",pn:175,y:"NEET 2019 \xB7 5 May",tp:"Equilibrium",st:"Buffer solutions",q:"Which will make basic buffer?",o:["100 mL of 0.1 M HCl + 200 mL of 0.1 M NH\u2084OH","100 mL of 0.1 M HCl + 100 mL of 0.1 M NH\u2084OH","50 mL of 0.1 M NaOH + 25 mL of 0.1 M CH\u2083COOH","100 mL of 0.1 M CH\u2083COOH + 100 mL of 0.1 M NaOH"],c:0,s:`A basic buffer is a weak base plus its salt, so the weak base must be left over after the strong acid is added.
Option 1: NH\u2084OH = 200 \xD7 0.1 = 20 mmol, HCl = 100 \xD7 0.1 = 10 mmol. After reaction 10 mmol NH\u2084OH is left with 10 mmol NH\u2084Cl: a basic buffer.
Option 2: 10 mmol each, so only salt (NH\u2084Cl) is left: no buffer.
Option 3: NaOH = 5 mmol, CH\u2083COOH = 2.5 mmol; the acid is used up and 2.5 mmol strong base is left over: not a buffer.
Option 4: 10 mmol each, only CH\u2083COONa is left: not a buffer.
Only option 1 is a basic buffer.
Note: Option 2 is printed 'NHOH' in the source; read as NH\u2084OH.`,sub:"Chemistry",ch:"Equilibrium"},{id:"neet-2019-q176",pn:176,y:"NEET 2019 \xB7 5 May",tp:"Structure of Atom",st:"Energy of orbitals",q:"4d, 5p, 5f and 6p orbitals are arranged in the order of decreasing energy. The correct option is :",o:["6p > 5f > 4d > 5p","5f > 6p > 4d > 5p","5f > 6p > 5p > 4d","6p > 5f > 5p > 4d"],c:2,s:`Compare (n + l): 4d = 4+2 = 6, 5p = 5+1 = 6, 5f = 5+3 = 8, 6p = 6+1 = 7.
Higher n + l means higher energy, so 5f > 6p > (5p, 4d).
When n + l is equal, the higher n has higher energy, so 5p > 4d.
Order: 5f > 6p > 5p > 4d.`,sub:"Chemistry",ch:"Structure of Atom"},{id:"neet-2019-q177",pn:177,y:"NEET 2019 \xB7 5 May",tp:"Chemistry in Everyday Life",st:"Antibiotics",q:"Among the following, the narrow spectrum antibiotic is :",o:["amoxycillin","chloramphenicol","penicillin G","ampicillin"],c:2,s:`Penicillin G acts mainly on gram-positive bacteria, so it is narrow spectrum.
Amoxycillin, ampicillin and chloramphenicol act on many types of bacteria (broad spectrum).`,sub:"Chemistry",ch:"Chemistry in Everyday Life"},{id:"neet-2019-q178",pn:178,y:"NEET 2019 \xB7 5 May",tp:"Aldehydes, Ketones and Carboxylic Acids",st:"Reactions of acids",q:`The major product of the following reaction is :
Phthalic acid (benzene-1,2-dicarboxylic acid) + NH\u2083, strong heating \u2192`,o:["2-aminobenzoic acid (ring with COOH and NH\u2082)","benzene-1,2-diamine (ring with two NH\u2082)","2-carbamoylbenzoic acid (ring with COOH and CONH\u2082)","phthalimide (ring fused to a five-membered ring C(=O)\u2013NH\u2013C(=O))"],c:3,s:`On strong heating, phthalic acid loses water to give phthalic anhydride.
Ammonia attacks the anhydride and opens the ring, forming the amide-acid, and on further heating this loses water and closes the ring.
The product is phthalimide.`,fig:{w:450,h:105,alt:"Four structures: (1) 2-aminobenzoic acid, (2) benzene-1,2-diamine, (3) 2-carbamoylbenzoic acid, (4) phthalimide, a benzene ring fused to a five-membered ring with C=O, NH and C=O.",d:[["t",0,56,"(1)",""],["p","M48 59.5 L35 67 L22 59.5 L22 44.5 L35 37 L48 44.5 Z",""],["l",43.8,58.6,36.3,62.9,""],["l",24.9,56.3,24.9,47.7,""],["l",36.3,41.1,43.8,45.4,""],["l",48,44.5,57.6,37.3,""],["t",78,34.5,"COOH","m"],["l",48,59.5,57.6,66.7,""],["t",74,77.5,"NH\u2082","m"],["t",115,56,"(2)",""],["p","M163 59.5 L150 67 L137 59.5 L137 44.5 L150 37 L163 44.5 Z",""],["l",158.8,58.6,151.3,62.9,""],["l",139.9,56.3,139.9,47.7,""],["l",151.3,41.1,158.8,45.4,""],["l",163,44.5,172.6,37.3,""],["t",189,34.5,"NH\u2082","m"],["l",163,59.5,172.6,66.7,""],["t",189,77.5,"NH\u2082","m"],["t",230,56,"(3)",""],["p","M278 59.5 L265 67 L252 59.5 L252 44.5 L265 37 L278 44.5 Z",""],["l",273.8,58.6,266.3,62.9,""],["l",254.9,56.3,254.9,47.7,""],["l",266.3,41.1,273.8,45.4,""],["l",278,44.5,287.6,37.3,""],["t",308,34.5,"COOH","m"],["l",278,59.5,287.6,66.7,""],["t",310,77.5,"CONH\u2082","m"],["t",345,56,"(4)",""],["p","M393 59.5 L380 67 L367 59.5 L367 44.5 L380 37 L393 44.5 Z",""],["l",388.8,58.6,381.3,62.9,""],["l",369.9,56.3,369.9,47.7,""],["l",381.3,41.1,388.8,45.4,""],["p","M393 44.5 L407.2 39.9 L407.2 39.9 L407.2 64.1 L393 59.5",""],["l",405.5,39.3,408.3,30.7,""],["l",409,40.4,411.7,31.9,""],["t",411.6,30.5,"O","m"],["l",409,63.6,411.7,72.1,""],["l",405.5,64.7,408.3,73.3,""],["t",411.6,81.5,"O","m"],["t",421.2,43.9,"NH","m"]]},sfig:{w:320,h:285,alt:"Phthalic acid on heating gives phthalic anhydride, which with H\u2082N\u2013H gives phthalimide.",d:[["p","M44.7 78.5 L30 87 L15.3 78.5 L15.3 61.5 L30 53 L44.7 61.5 Z",""],["c",30,70,9.86,""],["l",44.7,61.5,57,62.6,""],["t",66,66,"C","m"],["l",64.2,57,64.2,43,""],["l",67.8,57,67.8,43,""],["t",66,33,"O","m"],["l",71,62,78,62,""],["t",88,66,"O","m"],["l",93,62,104,62,""],["t",110,66,"H","m"],["l",44.7,78.5,57,77.4,""],["t",66,81,"C","m"],["l",67.8,82,67.8,96,""],["l",64.2,82,64.2,96,""],["t",66,114,"O","m"],["l",71,77,78,77,""],["t",88,81,"O","m"],["l",93,77,104,77,""],["t",110,81,"H","m"],["l",130,70,175,70,"a"],["t",152.5,64,"\u0394","ms"],["p","M224.7 78.5 L210 87 L195.3 78.5 L195.3 61.5 L210 53 L224.7 61.5 Z",""],["c",210,70,9.86,""],["l",224.7,61.5,235,62.5,""],["t",244,66,"C","m"],["l",242.2,57,242.2,43,""],["l",245.8,57,245.8,43,""],["t",244,33,"O","m"],["l",224.7,78.5,235,77.5,""],["t",244,81,"C","m"],["l",245.8,82,245.8,96,""],["l",242.2,82,242.2,96,""],["t",244,114,"O","m"],["t",270,74,"O","m"],["l",248,63,260.4,68.1,""],["l",248,77,260.4,71.9,""],["l",210,125,210,160,"a"],["t",218,146,"H\u2082N\u2013H",""],["p","M224.7 218.5 L210 227 L195.3 218.5 L195.3 201.5 L210 193 L224.7 201.5 Z",""],["c",210,210,9.86,""],["l",224.7,201.5,235,202.5,""],["t",244,206,"C","m"],["l",242.2,197,242.2,183,""],["l",245.8,197,245.8,183,""],["t",244,173,"O","m"],["l",224.7,218.5,235,217.5,""],["t",244,221,"C","m"],["l",245.8,222,245.8,236,""],["l",242.2,222,242.2,236,""],["t",244,254,"O","m"],["t",270,214,"N","m"],["l",248,203,260.4,208.1,""],["l",248,217,260.4,211.9,""],["l",275,210,282,210,""],["t",288,214,"H","m"],["t",210,272,"phthalimide","m"]]},sub:"Chemistry",ch:"Aldehydes, Ketones and Carboxylic Acids"},{id:"neet-2019-q179",pn:179,y:"NEET 2019 \xB7 5 May",tp:"Hydrogen",st:"Hardness of water",q:"The method used to remove temporary hardness of water is :",o:["Ion-exchange method","Synthetic resins method","Calgon\u2019s method","clark\u2019s method"],c:3,s:`Temporary hardness is due to Ca(HCO\u2083)\u2082 and Mg(HCO\u2083)\u2082.
Clark's method adds slaked lime: Ca(HCO\u2083)\u2082 + Ca(OH)\u2082 \u2192 2CaCO\u2083 + 2H\u2082O.
The insoluble carbonates are filtered off.`,sub:"Chemistry",ch:"Hydrogen"},{id:"neet-2019-q180",pn:180,y:"NEET 2019 \xB7 5 May",tp:"The d- and f-Block Elements",st:"Ores of metals",q:"Which one is malachite from the following?",o:["Fe\u2083O\u2084","CuCO\u2083\xB7Cu(OH)\u2082","CuFeS\u2082","Cu(OH)\u2082"],c:1,s:`Malachite is the green basic carbonate of copper, CuCO\u2083\xB7Cu(OH)\u2082.
Fe\u2083O\u2084 is magnetite and CuFeS\u2082 is copper pyrites.`,sub:"Chemistry",ch:"The d- and f-Block Elements"}]})();
